Release 1.3.23:
Billy G. - 2026-11-05

Upgrading:
* The spool holds the cached articles, the overview data and the posts
  waiting to be sent, and NewsCache creates its files there readable
  by others under the usual umask 022. Give the spool directory mode
  0750, owned by `Username` and `Groupname`, as the Debian package
  does: `chmod 0750 /var/cache/newscache`. The manual, newscache.conf(5),
  the sample configuration and FEDORA.md now say so.
* With `--without-syslog`, a new log file is created with mode 0640
  instead of 0644, so other local users can't read client addresses and
  user names in it. An existing log file keeps its mode: `chmod 0640` it.
* Numeric options take a plain number in a range now, and an AccessList
  prefix must be valid: "MaxConnections ten", "ClientTimeout 20m",
  "SpoolSize 10G" or "Client 192.168.1.0/", which were misread, are
  configuration errors with the file and line (see Bug fixes). Check the
  file with `newscache -p` before restarting.

Behaviour changes:
* A failed login (AUTHINFO PASS) waits at most 1 second before the 481,
  the same after each failure; the connection is still closed after the
  third. The delay was AuthFailureDelay times the number of failures (15,
  30 and 45 s by default, up to 3 hours with 3600), which only kept the
  session's process and memory busy. AuthFailureDelay now defaults to 1;
  larger values are accepted but count as 1, 0 still answers at once.
* PAM logins: NewsCache now gives PAM the client's address (PAM_RHOST), so
  host-based PAM rules (pam_access, pam_succeed_if rhost, ...) apply. They
  saw no host before: a service that allows only some hosts refused every
  login, and one that denies some hosts didn't. In inetd mode, and when
  the client's address can't be read, PAM gets no host. Check such rules
  before upgrading. configure --with-pam= (an empty service name) is now
  an error.
* After a failed AUTHINFO PASS (481), the pending USER is invalidated. A
  second PASS without AUTHINFO USER now gets 482 (RFC 4643 2.3.2); the client
  can retry by sending USER again.
* An AUTHINFO USER that is too long (481) also ends a pending USER, so a
  following PASS gets 482 instead of being checked against the earlier name.
* The log names the user in "failed authinfo for user <name>" only when that
  user's password was checked and refused. Other AUTHINFO failures (482, 501,
  502, an argument too long) are logged as "failed authinfo"; they were
  logged as a failed login of the last USER.
* A known command that the client's entry doesn't allow, where no login
  can help (Authentication none, or after the login), gets "502 access
  denied", and POST "440 posting not permitted" (RFC 3977). Both got
  "500 What?", the reply to an unknown command, which still gets 500.
* newscache -o (the configure options) no longer reads the configuration
  file, so it works when that is missing or has an error (it exited with
  2).
* XDEBUG dump authorization and dump configuration send their lines with
  CRLF, and dot-stuffed, as RFC 3977 wants; they ended with a bare LF.
* updatenews -l alone works as -g -l (prefetch the groups, each one
  locked); it prefetched nothing. -a alone still only posts the queue and
  refreshes the active database.
* configure without --prefix: the built-in configuration path is
  /usr/local/etc/newscache.conf (the default prefix), and make install puts
  the files there. It was the literal NONE/etc/newscache.conf, and make
  install wrote into ./NONE/etc.
* configure --without-pam (or --with-pam=no) no longer requires the PAM
  headers: on a host without them it stopped with "You don't have PAM
  header files", and with them it linked -lpam for nothing.
* configure --with-uniquename works: newscacheclean and updatenews then log
  under NewsCache's name. It never defined WITH_UNIQUE_PACKAGE_NAME (the
  test read a misspelled variable). newscache -o prints it as
  --with-uniquename (was --with-uniquenames).
* configure --with-md5auto / --without-md5auto are accepted as documented
  (configure knew only --with-md5_auto, and that one turned autodetection
  off, whatever its value). The default is unchanged. Note:
  without md5auto every stored hash is checked as MD5, so crypt() hashes
  in an auth file stop working.
* After SIGHUP, new sessions continue to use the startup NiceServer value;
  changing it now requires a restart instead of partly applying it.
* Message-IDs from clients are checked before NewsCache consults the spool
  or forwards a command or post upstream: those in commands must meet RFC
  3977's opaque-string rules and be at most 250 octets; posted Message-ID
  headers must also meet RFC 5536's stricter syntax. Invalid IDs are
  rejected; upstream articles with longer IDs can still be read by article
  number.
* LIST OVERVIEW.FMT names the byte and line counts `:bytes` and `:lines`, the
  form RFC 3977 8.4.2 prefers, instead of the older `Bytes:` and `Lines:`
  (which the RFC still allows for compatibility). The OVER fields themselves
  are unchanged.
* When an internal error (for example a damaged spool database) interrupts
  a multi-line reply that has already begun (XOVER, LISTGROUP, LIST, ...),
  NewsCache now closes the connection instead of ending the list with "."
  as if it were complete, the same as for an upstream failure in the middle
  of a list. Before anything is sent, the client still gets a 403.
* ConnectTimeout defaults to 30 seconds. It was 0, which leaves the
  connect to the system: a news server that doesn't answer (a firewall
  that drops the packets, a dead route) held each command that needed
  it about two minutes per address of the server, with no reply to the
  client, and the next command waited again. ConnectTimeout 0 keeps the
  old behaviour. ConnectBackoff stays 0; the manual now says that its
  time counts from the start of the failed attempt.
* Only one newscacheclean runs at a time, and only one updatenews that
  prefetches (-g or -o): a second run, as cron starts one when the first
  takes long, exits with status 3 and a message. Two newscacheclean runs
  that overlapped both computed the purge from the same sizes and together
  removed about twice what was needed; two prefetch runs fetched the same
  groups twice. The locks are the files .newscacheclean.lock and
  .updatenews.lock in the spool directory, held while the process runs.
  newscacheclean -t (try only) and updatenews -a alone don't take the
  lock.
* MODE READER and the greeting follow the client's rights (RFC 3977, RFC
  4643). MODE READER got "480 Authentication required" where the access
  entry allows only the login ("allow authentication"), and where a login
  is required ("Authentication unix" without fields) so did CAPABILITIES,
  although newsreaders send both before AUTHINFO (and must not send MODE
  READER after the login), so a newsreader couldn't learn from
  CAPABILITIES that it may log in; unknown commands got 480 there instead
  of 500. The greeting and MODE READER answered 200 (posting allowed) to
  every client; both now answer 201 (posting prohibited) when the client
  may not post at that moment: read-only entries, entries that allow only
  the login, and before a login that is required or that would add the
  post right. Such clients get 201 instead of 200 in the greeting; after
  the login, CAPABILITIES says whether POST is available.
* A failed login (a wrong password, an unknown user, a stored hash that
  can't be checked, a refusal by PAM, a user name or password longer
  than 511 characters) is now answered with 481 instead of 502, as RFC
  4643 says. 502 means that AUTHINFO isn't available at all; it stays
  the answer to AUTHINFO after a successful login and where the entry
  has no Authentication. A script that looks for 502 after a failed
  login must look for 481.
* POST answered with codes RFC 3977 doesn't allow after the article has
  been sent, where only 240 and 441 are: "480 authentication required"
  for a group outside PostTo while a login could add it, "440 posting
  not allowed", and "449 operation failed" (for example, when a post
  for an offline news server couldn't be spooled). All three are 441
  now; the first says "authentication required" in its text. A client
  that waited for 480 there to offer a login will show the 441 instead.
* newscacheclean used NiceServer for its priority instead of NiceClean,
  which had no effect. It uses NiceClean now (default 3), as
  newscache.conf(5) says; check the value if you set NiceServer.
* A changed `Hostname` takes effect only on a restart; it applied to the
  sessions started after a reload. A reload no longer looks up the host
  name (a DNS lookup that could stall the daemon), so the default name is
  looked up once, at startup, and only when the file sets no `Hostname`.
* A walk that gives up after 5 minutes without an article answers 403;
  after part of a list the connection is closed, as after other upstream
  failures. NEXT and LAST answer 403 then, instead of running until they
  find an article.

New options:
* ReadTimeout (in a Server entry): give up the connection to the news
  server when it sends or accepts nothing for this many seconds (each
  read and write; default 300, 0 waits for ever).
* MaxArticleSize: the largest article (bytes) taken from a news server
  (default 1048576). A larger one is refused: the client gets 403,
  nothing is stored and the connection to that server is closed.
* MaxPostSize: the largest article (bytes) a client may post (default
  1048576, 1 MB). A larger article is refused with 441 and the session
  goes on. There was no limit: a client could send an endless article (or
  line) until the session ran out of memory.
* TLS for clients (implicit TLS, RFC 8143; through GnuTLS). TLSListenTo
  opens a second listening socket (same form as ListenTo, default port
  563) next to the plain one, with TLSCertFile (PEM chain), TLSKeyFile
  and optionally TLSPriority (a GnuTLS priority string; a warning when it
  allows TLS 1.0 or 1.1). The certificate and key are loaded before
  newscache switches to Username, checked by newscache -p, and loaded
  again on SIGHUP (the old ones stay if that fails). The handshake must be
  done within ClientTimeout, at most 30 seconds also with ClientTimeout 0.
  newscache -t is inetd mode with TLS on the passed socket. An AccessList
  entry with "AuthRequiresTLS yes" answers AUTHINFO on a plain connection
  with 483 and lists AUTHINFO without USER in CAPABILITIES (RFC 4643).
  STARTTLS for clients is not offered. newscache -o lists --with-gnutls.
* `--enable-sysv-init` installs an optional Linux and GNU/Hurd init.d
  script, disabled by default; `--with-initdir` selects its installation
  directory. It also works where the daemon's directory is a symbolic link
  (/usr/sbin -> bin on Fedora and Arch).
* MaxConnectionsPerHost n (standalone mode): the most sessions one client
  host may have at a time, so that one host can't hold all of
  MaxConnections with idle connections. A host is an IPv4 address, or
  the /64 network of an IPv6 address. A client over the limit gets "400
  too many connections from your host" (on TLSListenTo it is
  disconnected without a reply), and the log says so, at most once a
  minute with a count. The default is 0, no limit, so clients behind one
  NAT address are not affected unless it is set.
* Server option "Options compress": COMPRESS DEFLATE (RFC 8054) to the
  news server. After STARTTLS, the login and MODE READER, NewsCache
  asks the server to compress the connection; from its 206 on,
  everything is compressed both ways, so articles, overview data and
  the active list come in a fraction of their size. A server that
  refuses it, or doesn't know COMPRESS, is used without compression
  (a notice is logged); invalid compressed data closes the connection.
  The login is never compressed: when the server asks for it again
  (480), NewsCache opens a new connection. It needs zlib: configure
  finds it (--with-zlib requires it, --without-zlib leaves it out), and
  "newscache -o" lists --with-zlib. zlib's development files are a new
  optional build dependency (zlib1g-dev, zlib-devel).

Bug fixes:
* LimitGroupSize was not applied to LIST ACTIVE entries. Until a GROUP reply
  refreshed the active database, LIST ACTIVE and cached GROUP results exposed
  the full range and updatenews -g could prefetch it. Active-list entries now
  get the same newest-N cut as GROUP replies.
* Authentication file: and pam+file: now warn at server start, and after a
  successful SIGHUP reload, when an auth file is writable by group or
  world (for a symbolic link: its target), and once when the file or the
  link target cannot be inspected. The warning includes the path and never
  blocks login; each configured path is reported once per configuration
  read.
* Malformed commands starting with "auth" could expose their arguments in
  the log, including passwords. The log now keeps only the command word for
  these commands, and the reused request buffer is cleared before each read.
* MD5 passwords in the auth file work on big-endian hosts (s390x, ppc64,
  sparc64, ...): the MD5 code never reversed its words there
  (HIGHFIRST was not defined), so every digest was wrong and no MD5
  password matched. configure now checks the byte order.
* When a configuration file could not be opened, newscache,
  newscacheclean, updatenews and cConfig reported "unexpected EOF". They
  now name the file and show the system error.
* newscacheclean -s now reports the number of cached group directories in
  DirCnt (always 0 before), and with -t the database and article counts
  and sizes found in the spool, leaving the purge counters at zero; try
  mode skipped the statistics and printed zeros for every field.
* Two sessions that started at the same time on a new spool could fail:
  the second one couldn't create the spool directories (EEXIST) and
  ended before its greeting.
* Error messages in the log showed a random line number ("Line:
  808335154") for errors raised without a location.
* A line of the upstream's active list without blanks (a group name
  alone) was read past its end, up to 512 bytes, before it was skipped.
* Over TLS, NewsCache didn't send QUIT to the upstream server when it
  closed the connection: the command stayed in the buffer.
* With "Options ssl", a server that closed the connection after the TLS
  handshake, before its greeting (an overloaded server), was never
  reached again in that attempt: the retries reused the TLS session of
  the closed connection, and every one of them failed.
* An element of a server's Read list longer than 512 characters
  overwrote the stack at the first refresh of the active list (the
  session crashed or hung).
* ARTICLE <message-id> with a message-ID of about 1000 characters, or a
  POST whose Message-ID was longer than about 500, hung the session for
  ReadTimeout (300 s): the command to the upstream server was cut off
  together with its line end, and the server waited for the rest. The
  same went for a very long upstream User or Password.
* With several upstream servers, ARTICLE <message-id> answered "503
  operation failed" when one of the servers asked before the one that
  had the article couldn't be reached. Such a server is now skipped.
* A damaged group database found while an overview reply from the
  upstream server was being stored left the rest of that reply unread:
  the following commands read those lines as their replies, so articles
  the server had were answered 423 and the session stayed out of step
  with the server. The connection is now closed and opened again.
* updatenews lost a spooled article without a Message-ID (servers with
  "Options dontgenmsgid") when the upstream server refused it or
  couldn't take it: logging its Message-ID failed, the run aborted, and
  the article was neither posted nor kept in .badArticles. An article
  whose posting fails with an unexpected error now stays in the spool.
* Posts kept for later (news server offline or unreachable) were moved to
  the spool's .badArticles directory, and never sent, when updatenews ran
  while the news server still couldn't be reached. They now stay in
  .artSpool until the server takes them, and the run goes on with the
  other articles; the log says how many were kept. Only articles the
  server refuses go to .badArticles. A spooled article is also no longer
  lost when updatenews is stopped while posting it.
* An upstream server could send an endless line or article, and the
  session read it until it ran out of memory. Articles are now limited by
  MaxArticleSize, and lines outside articles (status lines, overview
  records, the active list, LISTGROUP) to 16 KiB; more is an error, and
  the connection to the server is closed. A news server that sent an
  article, or the LIST OVERVIEW.FMT reply read when NewsCache connects,
  one byte at a time with pauses just under ReadTimeout could hold the
  session (and the locks it held) for hours. The article now has a limit
  for the whole reply, two minutes plus MaxArticleSize at 4 KB/s (6
  minutes for the default 1 MiB), and the overview format 30 seconds, 1000
  lines and 1 MiB; NewsCache then closes the connection and answers the
  client with 403.
* With very few free file descriptors (ulimit -n 5 on Linux, 6 on FreeBSD),
  or a spool where the lock file can't be created, a session aborted
  before the greeting instead of answering 400: the lock class threw a
  plain number that nothing caught.
* updatenews ignored the last line of the prefetch file when the file
  didn't end with a newline. A final "!pattern" was lost, so the groups
  it should leave out were prefetched.
* updatenews died with SIGABRT and a core dump, without a log line, when
  its spool couldn't be created or written. It now logs the error and
  exits with status 1.
* updatenews ignored ConnectTimeout: connecting to a server that doesn't
  answer took the system's timeout (about two minutes on Linux). And
  SIGPIPE, from a write to a connection the server had closed, ended the
  prefetch after the current group as if updatenews had been stopped;
  it is ignored now, as in newscache.
* updatenews -w (--workers) always failed with the usage text, so it
  neither posted nor prefetched anything. The option is now accepted
  and ignored: groups are prefetched one at a time. --lock-groups, as
  the usage text spells it, works too.
* XDEBUG: "xdebug dump" with an unknown argument got no reply, so the
  client waited for ever; it now gets 501. The replies of "xdebug dump"
  and "xdebug help" begin with the code 100 instead of "xxx" or no code
  at all.
* OVER, XOVER and XHDR without a range and without a current article (in
  an empty group) answered an empty list instead of "420 no current
  article" (RFC 3977); HDR already did.
* LIST ACTIVE with a wildmat showed groups outside the client's List:
  for a group that no List rule matched, the wildmat alone decided, so
  "LIST ACTIVE *" from a client with "List test.*" listed every group.
* LIST NEWSGROUPS sent every description in the spool's .newsgroups
  file, also those of groups outside the client's List, and LIST
  NEWSGROUPS with a wildmat (RFC 3977) answered 501. It now shows the
  groups of the client's List, and with a wildmat those that match.
* A command line of more than about 1020 characters closed the
  connection without a reply. It now gets "501 Line too long" and the
  session goes on; a line of 256 words or more gets 501 too, instead of
  500 (RFC 3977).
* The client's password was logged when its AUTHINFO line started with a
  blank or a tab: the log hid the arguments only of lines that began
  with "AUTHINFO", but the command itself skipped leading blanks. The
  line was logged at level info, and after a wrong password once more at
  level notice.
* With "LogStyle strict-inn" the connection wasn't closed after the
  third failed login, as the manual says it is, so a client could try
  passwords for ever on one connection (with "AuthFailureDelay 0" at
  full speed).
* AUTHINFO was accepted again after a successful login, and a second
  login added the second user's rights to the first one's; a failed one
  kept the first user's rights under the second user's name. It now gets
  502, as RFC 4643 says. A client whose entry has no Authentication gets
  502 at once too (it got 381, then 502 after the failure delay).
* Auth-file lines longer than 1023 bytes (a long List) were split
  silently: the line lost its later fields, such as the flags, and the
  rest was read as a line of its own, whose start was taken for a user
  name. Lines may be of any length now.
* A successful login wasn't logged with the user name (a failed one
  was), so the log didn't tell which account a session used; it is now
  logged at level notice ("authenticated as <user>"). The client's
  password is also cleared from memory right after the check instead of
  being kept for the whole session.
* Built with --without-syslog, newscache, newscacheclean and updatenews
  opened their log file as root, following symbolic links, in a
  directory the sample config said to make writable for the news user.
  That user could replace the log with a link to any file, which root
  then appended to (or created) with text partly chosen by clients. Now
  only a regular file with one link is used, and the error goes to
  stderr; a link to the program's own stderr, as in the container image,
  still works. The directory doesn't need to be writable for the news
  user when the programs start as root.
* An empty element in a group list, such as "LIST ACTIVE.TIMES ," from
  a client or empty List, Read or PostTo fields in the auth file, made
  NewsCache read past the end of two strings when it combined the lists
  (seen with AddressSanitizer). Empty elements are now skipped.
* Built with --without-syslog, the log lines of parallel sessions got
  mixed up: each line went out in three writes (time stamp, text,
  newline), and another session's line could land in between. Each line
  is now written at once.
* ARTICLE, HEAD and BODY by number answered "423 no such article" when
  the news server failed to send the article (a timeout, the connection
  closed in the middle, an article cut off), and some newsreaders then
  take the article as gone. The client now gets "403 internal error",
  the log says what went wrong, and 423 means the article doesn't exist.
* In groups of a not-cached server, XOVER, XHDR and the LISTGROUP
  fallback left out articles 1 to 9999 when the news server reported a
  low water mark of 0 (as some sloppy servers do): the group looked
  empty.
* A log message without a line end went out with the priority of the
  next message, which a syslog filter could drop: the error that
  posting couldn't spool an article was logged at the next message's
  level. It keeps its own priority now.
* configure --enable-headerlog gave a build that didn't compile (a typo
  since 2008), and newscache -o printed the option as
  --enable_headerlog.
* NEWGROUPS listed the new groups as "group time creator" (the format
  of LIST ACTIVE.TIMES). RFC 3977 wants the format of LIST ACTIVE,
  "group high low status", which newsreaders parse.
* newscacheclean followed symlinks in the spool directory. Through a
  link to a directory elsewhere it deleted files there (*.bad, .art*,
  files named like history shards) and replaced a file named .db, and a
  link loop made it count the spool many times over and purge articles
  for nothing. It now skips symlinks.
* In standalone mode a client got no reply when its session couldn't
  open the spool (missing or not writable): the "400 ... service not
  available" stayed in the buffer, because the session exited without
  sending it.
* A client whose host name has more than 63 characters (on Linux) was
  taken for its address: name and domain Client entries didn't match it,
  and the log and X-NNTP-Posting-Host showed the address. Names of up to
  255 characters are used now.
* In inetd mode (and when the client's address can't be read) the
  address entries of the access list were matched against an
  uninitialized address; a session could get the rights of such an entry
  instead of the "stdin" one, depending on what was on the stack.
* SIGTERM to the daemon left its sessions running until ClientTimeout
  (20 minutes by default): the daemon didn't pass it on, and a session
  noticed SIGTERM only after the client's next command, and not at all
  while it was blocked sending to a client that didn't read. The daemon
  now sends SIGTERM to its sessions, which end at once with "400 ...
  service discontinued", as newscache(8) says.
* The daemon could hang for good, accepting no more clients, when a
  client session ended with an error at the moment the daemon itself
  wrote a log line (after a reload, for example): the SIGCHLD handler
  wrote its own log line from inside the signal handler, which isn't
  safe. The handler now only records the ended session; the daemon logs
  it from its main loop.
* Started without -n, the daemon closed its standard input, output and
  error instead of pointing them at /dev/null, so the next files it
  opened took their place: in a session process the client's connection
  became the standard error, where error messages of the C library go.
  And a Username or Groupname that doesn't exist was reported only on
  that closed standard error: the daemon stopped at once without a word
  in the log. Such errors are logged now too.
* Started as root without Username, NewsCache and every client session
  kept running as root without a word. It now logs a warning.
* Built with --enable-plainpass, a locked auth-file line ("user:*",
  "user:!...") let in whoever sent the field itself as the password, and
  a hash worked as its own password: the plain-text comparison came
  first. The hash is now checked first, the plain comparison only for a
  field that is no hash, and a locked or empty field never matches.
* When the upstream server reset an idle connection (a firewall or load
  balancer timeout), the client's session ended with "400 ... service
  discontinued" after its next command, although NewsCache had already
  reconnected: SIGPIPE was handled like SIGTERM. It is ignored now.
* An upstream server that answered 503 (as INN does for data it doesn't
  keep) or 400 to LIST OVERVIEW.FMT or MODE READER made NewsCache
  reconnect again and again, thousands of times per session, until the
  session crashed. One that answered 500 to LIST OVERVIEW.FMT (old
  servers) made the first command of every session fail ("411 no such
  news group"). NewsCache now uses the standard overview format then, as
  it was meant to.
* An article that the upstream server cut off (the connection closed in
  the middle) was served as if complete and kept in the spool for good,
  so every later reader got the cut copy. The client now gets an error
  and the article is fetched again next time; a cut-off overview range
  or active list isn't taken as complete either.
* Malformed wildmat patterns from a client (XPAT, LIST ACTIVE), such as
  "a[b-" or a trailing backslash, were read past their end; they now
  match nothing.
* NEWGROUPS took a time given in GMT as local time on Linux, and used
  the daylight saving state of the current day instead of the given
  one, so it listed groups from the wrong time window (off by the UTC
  offset, or an hour).
* A damaged spool file whose free space list reached into stored
  records could hand those records' space out again, so a new article
  silently overwrote another one; the file is now moved aside as
  damaged instead.
* When a spool file grew and couldn't be mapped into memory again (an
  address space limit, ulimit -v or LimitAS=), the session kept the file's
  lock for good, so every other session that needed that group, the active
  list or the history hung, and its next access to the file crashed it.
  The locks are now given back (also the outer ones when the file grew
  while a nested lock was given up), a write under its own lock reports an
  error instead of crashing, and the file is mapped again at the next
  access.
* A signal (SIGTERM, SIGHUP, the client timeout's alarm) that came while
  a session waited to upgrade its lock on a spool file left the session
  without the lock it already had, so it went on reading the file
  unlocked; one that came while it went back to its outer lock made the
  next unlock fail ("Lock Stack underflow"). The lock state is now kept.
* A spool file left longer than its header says (a session killed while
  the file grew) was mapped into memory again, with a "wrong database
  size" log line at level Critical, at every lock in every session until
  the file grew again. It is now mapped once.
* Under a file size limit (ulimit -f, LimitFSIZE=), a session died with
  SIGXFSZ (and a core dump) the first time a spool file would grow past
  it, instead of passing the article on as on a full disk. newscache and
  updatenews ignore SIGXFSZ now, so the write fails and is handled.
* An active list that the upstream server cut off was still taken as
  complete in the default setup (LIST ACTIVE with a pattern): the groups
  after the cut kept old numbers and new groups were missing until
  Timeouts ran out. The list is now fetched again by the next session.
* An article of 15 KB or less that the spool kept but couldn't read back
  (one without a Message-ID, or with a header the overview couldn't
  parse) was taken for a damaged group database every time it was read:
  the group's whole cache was moved to .db.bad and fetched again. An
  article without a Message-ID is no longer stored, and a stored article
  that doesn't parse is dropped on its own and fetched again.
* When the file of an article over 15 KB was written again (it had been
  found empty or unreadable), a crash in the middle (SIGKILL, the OOM
  killer, a power loss) left the spool saying the article was complete,
  and the cut-off file was served from then on. The article now counts
  as stored only after its file is written.
* LIST ACTIVE, LIST ACTIVE.TIMES and NEWGROUPS read the active list
  without a lock. When another session added groups meanwhile (a refresh
  of the active list), a client that read its list slowly got only part
  of it, ending in "." as if complete; the sound active database was
  moved to .active64.bad and fetched again, so NEWGROUPS then listed
  every group as new. The list is now collected under the lock and sent
  after it is released.
* With "Options starttls", the upstream password could go out in plain
  text: a 480 reply to MODE READER, which comes before STARTTLS, made
  NewsCache log in right away (the re-login added in 1.3.22). Anyone in
  the middle could force it by injecting the 480. The login now always
  waits for STARTTLS, and MODE READER is sent again after the login,
  which also fixes servers that want the login before MODE READER (the
  connect failed on them with a second, refused login).
* NEXT and LAST crashed the session at the first missing article number
  in a group of a server with "Options not-cached".
* POST: an article cut off by the client (the connection ended before
  the final ".") was posted upstream as it was. It is now refused (441)
  and not posted.
* ClientTimeout now also limits each write to the client and each read
  inside a command. A client that stopped reading its replies stayed
  connected for good, and if it did so during XOVER, XHDR, HDR or
  LISTGROUP it kept the group locked, so every other session that needed
  that group hung. A client that stopped in the middle of a POST was
  never disconnected either.
* An upstream news server that stopped answering held its session for
  good, together with the locks it had: during the refresh of the active
  list that lock made every new session hang before the greeting, so the
  whole cache was down until a restart; during a fetch the group was
  locked for everyone. With ReadTimeout the connection is given up and
  opened again, also over TLS (where the wait was without limit even
  when closing the connection).
* A header folded over several lines was read with the line breaks and the
  white space that starts each continuation line removed: "Subject: foo"
  followed by " bar" read "foobar" in HDR, XHDR and XPAT (by message-ID,
  and by number once the article was in the spool), where the overview
  from the news server says "foo bar". Unfolding now removes only the line
  break and keeps the white space around it (RFC 5322 2.2.3), as the news
  server's overview has it, so a group's OVER line no longer changes once
  the article is in the spool. HDR, XHDR and XPAT also turn a TAB in a
  header into a space, as OVER does (RFC 3977 8.5.2). For a Newsgroups
  header this means a fold after the comma still separates two names, and
  a fold instead of a comma is white space in a name, which is refused,
  not a third group made of the two names joined.
* The PostTo check and the posting read a Newsgroups header differently.
  The check split it on commas and matched each name against the PostTo
  list, but the matcher stopped at white space, so "Newsgroups: a b" was
  checked as a post to a only. The posting then split on white space as
  well as commas and sent the article to the news servers of both a and
  b. A client whose PostTo denied b could so post to b's server (that
  server's own reading of "a b" decided what happened to the article).
  1.3.22 (#50) had reworked the check's split, but left the two parsers.
  Both now use one split: names are separated by commas, with optional
  spaces and tabs around them; a name with white space or a control
  character in it is refused with "441 invalid Newsgroups header", and
  such an article gives no Read right by message-ID either.
* AUTHINFO: the password hashes are compared in constant time, so the
  answer's timing no longer tells how many leading characters of a guess
  matched. A stored hash that crypt() can't use is refused with 481 and
  an error in the log, also on libxcrypt and musl, where crypt() returns
  the failure token "*0" instead of NULL (a stored "*0" matched it for
  any password). Built with --without-md5 but with MD5 autodetection, a
  24-character field ending in "==" let any password in; it now goes
  through crypt(). md5crypt's result was never freed; it is now.
* "Authentication pam+file": the rights of the lines that apply to a user
  (the user's own line, the lines @group of their groups, and the line *)
  were joined as strings without a comma, so "at.test" and "us.*" became
  "at.testus.*" and "post" twice "postpost": with the manual's example the
  user lost every group and the post right, and "*" plus "!alt.*" became
  "*!alt.*". The rights of each line are now added on their own. A user
  without a line of their own now gets the rights of "*" and of their
  groups, as the manual says; if no line applies, the login is refused as
  before.
* A newsgroup pattern longer than 512 bytes overflowed a stack buffer in
  the group filter. A client that may connect could send one with
  LIST ACTIVE.TIMES; with glibc's checks the session aborted, without them
  the buffer was overrun. Long patterns in the config and the auth files
  went the same way. Patterns now have no length limit.
* An Xref header (or Xref overview field) from the upstream with a blank
  after its last entry made the session loop for ever at full CPU while
  it held the group's lock: the client got no answer, every other session
  on that group hung, and only SIGKILL ended it.
* A SpoolSize of 1 TByte (1073741824 KBytes) or more made every
  newscacheclean run delete the whole spool: the limit was computed in a
  32-bit number and wrapped negative. Values from 2147483648 on were cut
  by the parser. SpoolSize is a 64-bit number now, and anything but a
  plain number of KBytes is a config error.
* Integer options were read without any check: "MaxConnections ten"
  became 0 and turned every client away, "ClientTimeout 20m" became 20
  seconds, "Retries -1" made NewsCache reconnect for ever to a failing
  server, and a ReadTimeout above 2147483 overflowed the wait in
  milliseconds. ClientTimeout, MaxConnections, the Nice values,
  Timeouts, GroupTimeout, LimitGroupSize, Retries, ConnectBackoff,
  ConnectTimeout and ReadTimeout now take a plain number in a range
  (see newscache.conf(5)); anything else is a config error with the
  file and line.
* The last line of the config file was ignored when the file didn't end
  with a newline: a final "MaxConnections 99" or "Username news" had no
  effect, without a message, and a final "}" gave a misleading syntax
  error. It is read like any other line now.
* newscache -p left out Hostname and a server's BindFrom, so its output,
  used as a config, lost them.
* An AccessList entry with an empty prefix length, such as
  "Client 192.168.1.0/" (a typo), was taken as /0 and let in every IPv4
  client, without a word. Prefix lengths of 65536 and more wrapped around
  (65536 became /0 as well), and a netmask with gaps (255.0.255.0) was
  cut at its first gap. All of these are configuration errors now.
* The pid file was opened as root following symbolic links, in a
  directory the manual page says to make writable for the news user. That
  user could replace it with a link to any file (/etc/shadow, say), which
  the daemon emptied and overwrote with its pid at the next start. Now
  only a regular file with one link is written; anything else is left
  alone with a warning.
* ./autogen stops with an error when autoreconf fails (autoconf, automake
  or libtool missing). It used to say "Next run ./configure" anyway, and
  the build failed one step later because there was no configure script.
* An article with a NUL byte, or a CR outside CRLF, from the news server
  could not be read ("423 no such article number") when the CR was in a
  header, or, once stored, made NewsCache take the group's spool database
  for damaged at every read: it was moved to .db.bad and all cached
  articles of the group were fetched again. A lone CR in the body, or
  inside an overview record, went to clients in ARTICLE, BODY, OVER, XOVER
  and XHDR, and a reader that ends a line at a CR saw the reply end early
  and status lines chosen by the news server. Such bytes now become a
  space (RFC 5536 allows neither), when the article or record is read from
  the news server and when one that an older version stored is sent.
* XHDR Xref and HDR Xref killed the session (an uncaught exception,
  SIGABRT) when an overview record from the upstream server had an empty
  Xref field followed by a tab. They also answered ": host group:n"
  instead of "host group:n" from an overview record.
* A tab in a header (such as the Subject) of an article that a client
  had read shifted the fields of its overview record: XOVER showed the
  wrong byte and line counts, and STAT, NEXT and LAST answered with the
  Date instead of the message-ID. Tabs now become spaces, as RFC 3977
  says.
* A post that NewsCache keeps for later (with the news server offline or
  unreachable) was answered "240 Article posted" even when it could not
  be written to the spool (a full disk): the article was lost, or
  updatenews later posted it cut off. POST now answers with an error
  (441), and nothing half written is left in the spool.
* XHDR, XPAT and HDR with a header name longer than 511 characters could
  read past the end of an article that has a header starting with the
  same 511 characters, and send bytes from the session's memory to the
  client. Header names are now compared in full.
* POST through the daemon: an empty X-NNTP-Posting-Host header from the
  client made NewsCache drop the header after it as well (the post was
  refused when that was Newsgroups), a second X-NNTP-Posting-Host from
  the client went upstream next to NewsCache's own, and a header with a
  blank continuation line at the end of an article without body was
  read past its end. NewsCache now removes every X-NNTP-Posting-Host of
  the client and adds its own.
* XOVER, XHDR lines and HDR :lines showed the Lines header of the
  article, if it had one, instead of the number of body lines once a
  client had read the article; before that they showed the count from
  the news server. They now always show the number of body lines.
* When the news server failed (it couldn't be reached, closed the
  connection in the middle, didn't answer within ReadTimeout, or sent
  an article larger than MaxArticleSize), and on other internal errors,
  the client got 503, RFC 977's "program fault". RFC 3977 made 503
  "feature not supported" and has 403 for an internal fault. ARTICLE,
  HEAD, BODY, STAT, XHDR, XPAT and HDR by message-ID now answer "403
  upstream failure" instead of "503 operation failed", and any command
  that fails this way before it has sent anything (ARTICLE, HEAD and
  BODY by number, LIST, ...) answers "403 internal error" or "403 spool
  database damaged, try again" instead of 503. HDR still answers 503
  for a field it can't serve, as RFC 3977 asks.
* AUTHINFO PASS without a preceding AUTHINFO USER got 481 instead of
  482 (out of sequence, RFC 4643) when the password was longer than 511
  characters: the length was checked first. The sequence is now checked
  first. AUTHINFO with an unknown subcommand and a long argument now
  gets 501 (syntax) instead of 481.
* Where a login is required ("Authentication unix" without fields),
  CAPABILITIES listed READER, HDR, OVER, LIST and POST before the
  login, although all of them got "480 Authentication required" then.
  It now lists only what the client may do at that moment (RFC 3977,
  RFC 4643): AUTHINFO USER before the login, the reading commands and
  POST after it.
* ARTICLE, HEAD, BODY and STAT by number, NEXT, LAST, LISTGROUP, XOVER,
  OVER, XHDR, XPAT and HDR answered "412 operation failed" when the
  current group couldn't be opened (for example, its database in the
  spool can't be opened). 412 means "no newsgroup selected" in RFC 3977;
  the reply is now "403 operation failed" (internal fault). 412 is still
  the answer when no group is selected.
* NEWGROUPS answered "410 operation failed" when the active list couldn't
  be read; 410 is no NEWGROUPS reply. It now answers like LIST: "403
  internal error" (RFC 3977), after one more try on a damaged database.
* DATE would answer "500 gmtime failed" if the system couldn't convert
  the time; 500 means "unknown command". It is "403 gmtime failed" now
  (RFC 3977: internal fault). This doesn't happen in practice.
* GROUP and LISTGROUP answered "411 no such news group" also when the
  news server couldn't be reached or answered GROUP with an error, for a
  group the spool didn't know yet: the group looked as if it didn't
  exist. They now answer "403 internal error" then (RFC 3977); 411 is
  left for groups that don't exist. A group the spool knows is still
  served from it while the news server is down.
* A 503 reply of the news server was taken for a closed connection, and
  the command was sent again on a new connection, up to Retries times,
  before it failed. In RFC 3977, 503 means "feature not supported": a
  news server that answered OVER with 503 cost several connections for
  every overview request, and NewsCache never fell back to XOVER. A 503
  is now an ordinary reply, except when the server has closed the
  connection behind it, as old INN versions did with "503 Timeout" on
  an idle connection.
* When the current group no longer existed (the news server answered
  411 for it), ARTICLE, HEAD, BODY and STAT by number, NEXT, LAST,
  XOVER, OVER, XHDR, XPAT, HDR and LISTGROUP without a group answered
  "411 no such news group". RFC 3977 asks for 412 when the selected
  group is invalid, and keeps 411 for a group named in the command; the
  reply is now "412 current group no longer exists".
* TLS connections to upstream servers: a read or write interrupted by
  SIGTERM or the alarm was retried, so a session waiting for an upstream
  TLS server ended only when the server answered; now it ends at once,
  as with a plain connection.
* ListenTo (and TLSListenTo) with an IPv6 address such as [::1] failed on
  a host without a global IPv6 address, a container for example:
  getaddrinfo was asked for AI_ADDRCONFIG, which ignores loopback
  addresses. The listen address is numeric and is now used as given.
* The listening sockets queued at most 4 connections that weren't
  accepted yet; a burst of clients overflowed that, and FreeBSD reset
  the extra connections. The queue now holds 128 (MaxConnections still
  limits the sessions).
* newscache didn't check whether listen() worked on its listening
  sockets. On Linux two sockets can bind the same address and port, so
  with TLSListenTo on the port of ListenTo (or of another program) only
  listen() failed, and the daemon ran without that listener: TLS clients
  reached the plain listener. It now exits with "can't listen on
  TLSListenTo ...".
* When header logging is enabled, failures to open `.headerlog` are now
  logged instead of silently dropping the header.
* configure --with-gnutls now fails when the GnuTLS library or headers
  are missing, instead of silently building without TLS. With no explicit
  option GnuTLS remains optional, and a missing dependency is warned about.
* A termination or reload signal arriving just before the daemon entered
  poll could leave it waiting until another client connected. Signals now
  wake the idle daemon immediately.
* The default configuration path expands `${prefix}` in a custom
  `--sysconfdir` (it stayed literal, so a `--sysconfdir='${prefix}/conf'`
  build looked for a file under a directory named `${prefix}`). Shell
  syntax such as `$(...)` in `--prefix` or `--sysconfdir` stays literal
  and is not run.
* When a listener cannot bind, the final error on stderr and in the log
  now includes the system error, such as "Address already in use".
* SIGHUP reports each changed restart-only setting against the running
  startup configuration, including whether ListenTo was specified. Notices
  repeat until restart or until the configured setting is reverted.
* OVER answers 423 when the requested article range is empty, including a
  reversed range, instead of sending an empty 224 response (RFC 3977).
* OVER by message-ID now answers 503, because this optional form is not
  supported, whether or not a newsgroup is selected (RFC 3977).
* Generated Message-IDs no longer exceed the 250-octet limit with a long
  Hostname: when necessary, the host part is the hostname's MD5 in lowercase
  hexadecimal, while the time, process and counter components are retained.
* Reject overlong database paths before copying them into the fixed-size
  container filename buffer, without disturbing an already-open database.
* A configuration path that is a directory is now reported as "cannot open
  configuration file <path>: Is a directory" instead of "unexpected EOF".
* A login with an unknown user (or a locked line) in `Authentication file`
  or `Authentication unix` is no longer answered faster than one with a
  wrong password. A dummy hash check takes the same time, so the time of
  the reply no longer tells which user names exist.
* updatenews now retains group-number information for up to 60 seconds during
  prefetch, avoiding redundant upstream GROUP commands within a prefetch.
* Upstream TLS handshakes now have a whole-handshake deadline based on
  ReadTimeout, or ConnectTimeout when ReadTimeout is zero (60 seconds when
  both are zero), and stop promptly on termination signals. STARTTLS retains
  the configured timeout after upgrading.
* CAPABILITIES now advertises LIST EXTENSIONS, which the client already
  implements.
* NEWGROUPS now rejects arguments beyond its optional GMT token, reports the
  correct syntax, and uses the RFC 3977 231 reply text without a timestamp.
* updatenews now logs an error with the reason (for example "No such file or
  directory" or "Permission denied") and exits nonzero when a configured
  PrefetchFile cannot be opened or is a directory or a FIFO (a directory was
  read as empty, a FIFO blocked the run). An empty PrefetchFile, or
  /dev/null, still means no groups to prefetch.
* NewsCache now warns in its log, at startup and after a reload, when a
  PAM service named by `PAMServicename` for `Authentication pam` or
  `pam+file` has no configuration in `/etc/pam.d` or `/etc/pam.conf`. PAM
  then silently uses its `other` service. The warning is advisory; logins
  are unchanged.
* NEWGROUPS rejects invalid calendar and clock values instead of allowing
  the C library to normalize them. Documented that NEWGROUPS and LIST
  ACTIVE.TIMES use the local first-seen time, not upstream creation times.
* Free the last selected group and the server object when a client session
  ends, and the purge list in `newscacheclean`, so that a leak-checking
  (LeakSanitizer) run of the test suite reports nothing.
* Bound upstream active lists, overview replies and LISTGROUP replies so a
  broken server cannot keep consuming resources or holding locks
  indefinitely: an active list may have at most 2,000,000 lines and 256
  MiB and take at most 5 minutes, an overview or LISTGROUP reply as many
  lines as the requested range and at most 2 minutes. Incomplete replies
  are not marked fresh, and their upstream connections are closed rather
  than reused. An incomplete combined active list is retried after at most
  60 seconds, rather than on every request. Interrupted overview and
  LISTGROUP replies are reported as failures rather than as a successful
  empty or incomplete range.
* Filter Xref headers in one pass so a large crosspost list from an upstream
  server cannot hold the group lock for quadratic time.
* A client that stops reading its replies now ends its session after one
  ClientTimeout. Output left in the send buffer after the failed send was
  sent again by every later flush, each waiting for the timeout once more,
  so such a session lasted several ClientTimeouts (seen with TLS on
  EL9).
* A client's LISTGROUP goes to the news server in steps, each with its
  own reply limits: 10000 numbers at a time in a dense group, larger
  steps (up to 100000000 numbers) across gaps; it fails when no number
  reached the client for 5 minutes. It used to be one request
  for the whole range, or the whole group, so a list of millions of
  numbers could run into the reply deadline, and a provider that
  answers 503 to large ranges made NewsCache fetch overview records
  instead.
* Posts forwarded to multiple upstream servers now get missing Date and
  Injection-Date headers before forwarding, so each injector receives the
  same dates along with the shared Message-ID.
* An inetd session (newscache -i) that gets SIGINT or SIGTERM between two
  commands ends at once with "400 ... service discontinued"; it waited for
  the client's next line. Reads inside a command, such as the article of a
  POST, now also give up after ClientTimeout plus 10 seconds in inetd
  mode, as they do in the daemon; a stalled POST kept the session forever.
* Bytes of 0x80 and above in client commands, newsgroup names and the
  configuration file are passed to the C character class functions as
  unsigned char. As plain (signed) char they were negative values,
  undefined for those functions, and read outside the table on C
  libraries that index it directly.
* POST refuses an article with a NUL or a CR outside CRLF (441), as
  RFC 5536 requires. It was forwarded as it came: against a news server
  that ends a line at a lone CR, the article ended early and the rest
  ran as commands on NewsCache's own connection to that server. A
  spooled article with such a CR, from an older version, is posted
  with the CR replaced by a space.
* Control bytes in a log line (ESC, a CR, DEL and the other bytes below
  0x20) are now written as "?". Client input went into the log as it
  came, in commands, user names, group names and host names from DNS, so
  a client could make a line look like something else on a terminal or
  split it for a log parser. The same applies to the article headers that
  configure --enable-headerlog writes to .headerlog, except tabs and the
  line ends.
* An article over 15 kB is stored in a file of its own. NewsCache wrote
  it in place: a crash or power cut could leave the file cut off, and
  NewsCache then served it without its last partial line and without a
  word; a write that failed (a full disk) removed the copy that was
  there. Now the file is written under a temporary name (.art<n>.tmp),
  flushed to the disk (fsync) and renamed, and the directory is flushed
  too, before the article counts as stored: the file is either whole or
  the old one. A cut-off file from an older version, whose last line has
  no line end or that has no empty line after the header, is fetched
  again from the news server and written anew. If the news server
  doesn't have the article any more, the stored copy is served as it is
  and the log says so; the file is kept (one cut before its Message-ID
  header can't be served and is removed, as before). (A file cut right
  after a line end in the body can't be told from a whole one.) Complete
  files with LF line ends (as older versions or an admin may have
  written them) are served as before. newscacheclean counts a .tmp file
  that a crash left behind as an article file and removes it by its age.
* A session or updatenews stopped with SIGTERM or SIGINT (updatenews also
  with SIGHUP, SIGUSR1 or SIGUSR2) while it waited for the news server
  opened new connections to it before it ended: the connect was retried
  (Retries times, a second apart), a command was sent again after a
  reconnect, and updatenews tried the next server and fetched the active
  list. With a ConnectTimeout (it is 30 by default now), the reconnect
  even cleared the signal's mark, so the session carried on until the next
  command or ReadTimeout; with ConnectTimeout 0, an interrupted connect to
  a news server that didn't answer was started again and waited for the
  system's timeout (minutes). Now nothing is retried and no reconnect is
  made after the signal (a connect that began at that very moment is
  closed before a command is sent on it), and a wait for the news server
  ends within about a second: a signal that came just before a wait began
  (after the check for it) is caught by an alarm every second until the
  process ends. The reply "service discontinued" gets two seconds, and a
  TLS send isn't started again once the signal is seen. The log says
  "given up: shutting down" or "not read to its end: shutting down"
  instead of blaming the news server, and an empty line for a greeting
  that never came is no longer logged.
* Opening a group's database waits for its lock and then checks the
  file. A signal that interrupted the wait (SIGTERM, or SIGHUP for
  updatenews) let the checks run without the lock: they could see
  another process half way through a change and call a sound database
  corrupt, which moved it aside and started an empty one. Now the wait
  goes on after a signal, and a lock that can't be taken is an error.
  The same holds for the directory lock taken before a corrupt database
  is moved aside.
* The lock of the post spool (.artSpool) gave up when a signal
  interrupted the wait for it: after SIGTERM, a post that the news
  server didn't take could fail to reach the spool, and the client got
  an error. Now the wait goes on; the lock is held only while one file
  is stored or removed.
* A client that asked for a long XOVER, XHDR or LISTGROUP reply and stopped
  reading held the group's shared lock until its send timed out
  (ClientTimeout), and every session that had to store an article or an
  overview record for that group waited that long - 600 seconds with the
  sample configuration. The records are now formatted under a short lock and
  written with the lock released: at most 64 records or 64 KB for XOVER and
  XHDR, and at most 4096 numbers or 64 KB for LISTGROUP.
* Fetching an article from the news server held the group's shared lock for
  the whole fetch (bounded by ReadTimeout, 300 seconds by default), so every
  session that needed to store an article or an overview record for that group
  waited for the news server. The article is now fetched with the lock
  released and stored afterwards; an article that another session stored, or a
  group that was resized, meanwhile is handled when the article is stored.
* A daemon session (not one started by inetd) stopped with SIGTERM or
  SIGINT while a command ran, for example while it waited for the news
  server, sent neither that command's reply nor "400 ... service
  discontinued": the signal made the send fail, and the log said "can't
  be written to, session ended". A client whose post had gone to the
  spool got no 240 and could post it again. Now the reply and the 400 go
  out, with two seconds for both.
* A client's LIST ACTIVE (or GROUP, when it needs the group's entry) that
  found the active list stale held the list's exclusive lock while NewsCache
  fetched the list from the news servers, so every other session's GROUP
  waited for the news server - up to the whole-reply limit of 5 minutes per
  server. The list is now fetched without the database's lock and stored
  afterwards: a fetch that fails midway (reply limit, deadline) leaves the
  stored list as it was, while a reply cut off in the middle still stores the
  lines that were read, as before. A second lock in the spool
  (.active.refresh) makes sure only one session fetches the list at a time: a
  session that finds another one fetching serves the list it has, and only a
  session with no list at all (first start, a damaged .active64) or
  updatenews -a waits for the fetch to finish. Meanwhile a group that is new
  on the news server is unknown (411) until that fetch is done. A session or
  updatenews stopped with SIGTERM or SIGINT while it waits for another
  one's fetch ends at once; it waited for that fetch to finish. A session
  stopped in the middle of its own fetch no longer marks the news servers
  as failed, which made the other sessions serve the old (or an empty)
  list for up to a minute.
* A client's XOVER, OVER, XHDR or HDR on a range of a group that
  NewsCache hadn't synchronized yet (or not within GroupTimeout) held the
  group's exclusive lock while it asked the news server for the group's
  numbers, the article list and the overview records. Every other session
  that read that group, even an article already in the spool, waited for
  the news server: up to 2 minutes per 10000 article numbers, longer with
  a server that sends slowly. The news server is now asked without the
  lock, and the records are stored afterwards under a short one. A new
  lock file in the spool (.fetch.lock, no data in it) still lets only one
  session fetch a range at a time: a second session that wants the same
  range waits for the first one and serves what it stored, instead of
  asking the news server again.
* updatenews -g held the group's lock while it asked the news server for
  the article list and each article, so clients reading the group waited
  for it (with -l, for the whole prefetch of each 10000 numbers). It now
  locks the group only while it stores or removes an article. With -l a
  range is still reserved for the prefetch while it runs: sessions that
  need its overview wait for the prefetch instead of fetching it too, but
  sessions reading what the spool holds no longer wait.
* A stored article that can't be read is removed from the cache, so that
  it is fetched again. When another session had just stored a good copy of
  that article, the good copy could be removed instead (and was fetched
  again on the next request). Now only the unreadable copy is removed.
* The first fetch of the active list (the list of groups) on a new spool,
  from several news servers or with several Read patterns, no longer gives
  other sessions a partial list while it runs: they wait for it. If NewsCache
  was stopped during that first fetch, the next session fetches the list
  again; before, the part already fetched was served as complete until the
  list timed out (Timeouts).
* When newscacheclean prunes history for a group whose spool database is
  gone, it no longer removes a location that another session has updated or
  whose group has since been recreated. Losing that location made a
  message-ID lookup go to the news server instead of finding the cached
  article (the article was still in the spool; only the lookup by
  message-ID missed it).
* When a group has an article under a new number, removing an old spool
  record or a stale history location no longer removes the new number from
  history. The cached article remains findable by message-ID instead of
  requiring a fetch from the news server.
* Reading an older cached copy of an article no longer replaces the history
  location of another copy in the same group. This keeps the surviving copy
  findable by message-ID when the older one is removed from the spool.
* A GROUP that gets a group's numbers from the news server no longer sets
  the group's posting flag in the active list to "y". A moderated ("m") or
  read-only ("n") group was listed as open for posting by LIST ACTIVE and
  NEWGROUPS until the active list was fetched again.
* The flag that NewsCache's and updatenews' signal handlers set to end a
  wait (alarmed) is now volatile, as C and C++ require for a variable a
  signal handler writes. A compiler was free to keep the old value in a
  register, and a loop could miss a timeout or SIGTERM.
* On systems where a lock held by another process makes fcntl report
  EACCES (POSIX allows it), updatenews no longer stops posting the spooled
  articles with an error when another process is posting them; it says
  that another process posts them, as on Linux and the BSDs.
* updatenews -g no longer marks a chunk of a group's overview as up to date
  for numbers it didn't fetch: in a group of more than 10 million numbers,
  the ones just below the prefetched part (in the same chunk of 10000), and
  numbers another session's range had already moved past while the
  prefetch ran. Clients asking for those numbers got nothing from the spool
  instead of the news server's overview.
* newscacheclean no longer removes a big article's file that a session
  stored while the cleaner was reading the group's directory: it took the
  file for one outside the group's range and removed it at the end of the
  run, even when the spool was below SpoolSize. Nor does it remove a file
  that was replaced under the same name since the scan (an article stored
  again, a group database started anew after a damaged one was moved
  aside): the check and the unlink are done under the lock the session
  takes for that change.
* When a group's article range changes during an overview fetch, NewsCache
  no longer marks an out-of-range fetch as synchronized. If the index later
  grows down into a partly covered chunk, its synchronization mark is cleared
  so missing overview records are fetched instead of treated as fresh.
* With "Options starttls", a reply shorter than a status code to MODE
  READER, STARTTLS or LIST OVERVIEW.FMT (which anyone in the middle can
  send), or a refused login, left the half set-up connection to the news
  server open. The next command went out on it in plain text, without
  STARTTLS or the login, and a 480 to that command made NewsCache send
  User and Password in plain text. Such a connection is now closed, and
  the next command connects again.
* An article with bare LF line ends, written as its body only, started
  with an extra empty line and ended with a stray NUL byte. The body is
  now written as it is.
* On FreeBSD and macOS a news server with BindFrom could not be reached
  ("Unable to bind to outgoing address"): bind() was given the size of
  a whole sockaddr_storage as the address length. It now gets the
  length of the address (IPv4 or IPv6).
* HELP showed a wildmat argument for NEWGROUPS, which the command
  rejects with 501, and a time argument for LIST ACTIVE.TIMES, which
  takes a wildmat. HELP and the 501 syntax reply now show what the
  commands accept; AUTHINFO, MODE and XDEBUG are listed completely.
* LIST NEWSGROUPS left out the last description when the .newsgroups
  file did not end with a newline. It is now listed.
* In a Server block of the configuration, a PostTo line before the Read
  line was overwritten by Read. PostTo now stays as given; without it,
  it still defaults to Read. The manual says so.
* A temporary error while reading a group's record (no free file
  descriptor, a lock timeout) made the lookup of an article by
  Message-ID forget where it was stored. The location now stays in the
  history unless its record is really gone or holds another article.
* The wildmat of LIST ACTIVE, LIST ACTIVE.TIMES and LIST NEWSGROUPS is
  now a list as in RFC 3977 section 4.2: comma separated patterns, each
  optionally led by "!", the last one that matches deciding. A list such
  as "comp.*,alt.*" or "comp.*,!comp.os.*" used to list nothing, and
  LIST ACTIVE.TIMES read its argument as an access rule list, so
  "LIST ACTIVE.TIMES *.test" listed every group the client may list.
* A client that posted two articles without a Message-ID in one session,
  to more than one server, within the same second could lose the second:
  both got the same generated Message-ID (the counter in it was always
  1), so the second was taken for a duplicate and dropped, while the
  client got 240. The counter now counts.
* A client that sent the article after POST slowly enough to never hit
  the per-read timeout (a byte every few seconds) could hold a session,
  and with enough of them all of MaxConnections, for ever. The whole
  article must now arrive within ClientTimeout after the 340 reply;
  otherwise the client gets "400 ... service timed out!" and the
  connection is closed.
* A client whose PostTo allowed only some groups could add a second
  Newsgroups header with another group: only the first was checked, and
  both went to the news server. A POST with a repeated Newsgroups, From,
  Subject, Date, Path or other header that RFC 5536 allows once is now
  refused with 441 "more than one ... header" and logged.
* When accept() failed because the daemon had run out of file
  descriptors or memory (EMFILE, ENFILE, ENOBUFS, ENOMEM), it retried at
  once, without pause: it used a whole CPU and logged "accept failed"
  for every try. It now logs the failure once, waits a second between
  tries (a signal still acts at once) and logs again after an accept
  that worked.
* Started without -n, newscache forked and its parent exited with status
  0 at once. If the daemon then could not listen (port in use) or drop
  its privileges, it said so only on a standard error already pointed
  at /dev/null: the start command reported success and no daemon ran.
  The parent now waits until the daemon listens; if the daemon ends
  before that, it exits with status 1 and the daemon's message is shown
  on the terminal.
* A C++ standard exception (std::bad_alloc, std::length_error ...) in a
  command ended the session without a reply, and in inetd mode (-i, -t)
  ended the process with SIGABRT and a core dump. The client now gets
  "403 internal error" like for NewsCache's own errors and the session
  goes on; the exception is logged. Outside a command the session ends
  with a log message.
* On a TLS connection to a news server, silence lasted twice as long as
  ReadTimeout before NewsCache gave up on the reply. Now it is one
  ReadTimeout, as on a plain connection.
* A session inherited the master's SIGCHLD handler, which reaps any child
  with waitpid. A helper process started by a PAM module during login
  could lose its exit status to it. Sessions, and the inetd mode, now
  leave SIGCHLD at its default.
* After a power cut or a crash, an article that a client had POSTed
  while the news server was unreachable ("240 Article posted") could be
  an empty file in the outgoing spool, and updatenews moved it to
  .badArticles: the post was lost. The article is now written to disk
  (and the directory synced) before the client gets the 240. A
  temporary file left in the outgoing spool by a crash is now removed
  by updatenews.
* One failure to open a history shard (too many open files, no space
  left while the file was created) switched the history off for that
  shard until the session ended: ARTICLE <message-id> went to the news
  server and the stored locations went stale. The shard is now tried
  again after a few seconds.
* An Article object that had not read or been given a text yet pointed
  at uninitialised memory when asked for its text. No code path did
  that, but a failed read (out of memory) could leave the same
  dangling pointer. The text pointer is now set in every constructor
  and when a read fails.
* After a process was killed while it grew a database file, the next
  growth left part of the new space unusable (up to 64 KB, more for
  large indexes). The free space is now counted from the size the
  header records.
* When a damaged database was moved aside and the new one could not be
  started because memory ran out, the lock on the spool directory
  stayed held until the process ended, and other processes that had to
  move a damaged database aside waited for it. The lock is released
  and the database is started again at the next use.
* A damaged record in the active database that was a few bytes longer
  than a group entry (554 to 560 bytes) was not recognised as damage:
  GROUP and the other commands for that group failed with an error
  until the file was removed by hand. The file is now moved aside and
  rebuilt, as for any other damaged record.
* updatenews kept the database of a group open when its prefetch failed
  with an unexpected error, one file descriptor and one mapping per group,
  until the run ended. A long run over many failing groups could run out
  of file descriptors. The group is now released.
* A group's last article number could go back: a GROUP reply stored after
  a newer one from another session put the older numbers back, and so
  could an active list that was still being read (it is now fetched
  without the database's lock, see above). For up to GroupTimeout, GROUP
  then showed the old last number, and the overview records and articles
  above it that had been cached were thrown away. A group's numbers now
  carry the time they were asked for, and older numbers no longer replace
  newer ones (in the same second, the higher last number stays). The
  list's posting flag is still taken over.
* Going back from an exclusive lock on a spool database to the shared
  lock held around it (as when a session removes an unreadable article
  while it reads a group) gave the lock up for a moment, and another
  process could change or resize the file in the middle of the shared
  section. The exclusive lock is now turned into the shared one at once;
  only taking an exclusive lock still gives the shared lock up first.
* A client could get "403" for a command that opened a group at the
  moment newscacheclean removed that group's directory (after its
  database): the session had made sure the directory was there, and
  then found it gone when it created the database. The session now
  makes the directory again and creates the database.
* A spooled article that updatenews could not read (one too big for the
  memory) ended the posting run with the lock of the outgoing spool
  still held: until updatenews ended, every session that spooled a POST
  while the news server was down waited, and the article, read first,
  stopped every later run as well. It is now moved to .badArticles, with
  a line in the log, and the next spooled article is posted.
* When a session ran out of memory while it sent overview data, header
  lines or article numbers of a group (OVER, HDR, LISTGROUP), or while
  it changed the active database and the disk was full, it kept the
  group's (or the active database's) lock until it ended, and every
  other session that had to change it waited. The lock is now given
  back in every case.
* The lookup of a client's host name ran before the greeting, with no
  time limit: a client whose reverse DNS zone doesn't answer kept a
  session, one of MaxConnections, for the resolver's timeouts (a minute
  and more). The lookup now takes at most 10 seconds (or ClientTimeout,
  if that is shorter); after that the client goes by its address, and
  the log says so.
* A client turned away at MaxConnections ("400 too many users") was not
  logged, so a server whose sessions were all taken (by a flood, or by
  idle clients) showed nothing. The log now names the first client
  turned away, counts the others at most once a minute while the limit
  is reached, and says when a session can be started again.
* Built with --without-syslog, the daemon wrote into its log file until
  it ended: after log rotation renamed newscache.log, it and every new
  session went on writing into the renamed file, so only copytruncate
  (which loses lines) worked. On SIGHUP the daemon now opens
  newscache.log again; sessions started afterwards write to the new
  file, running ones to the old one until they end. newscache.conf(5)
  and the manual have a logrotate example.
* The client's name, which its reverse zone gives (whoever has the
  address block runs that zone), went into X-NNTP-Posting-Host and the
  log as it was. musl's getnameinfo returns CR and LF too, so on musl
  systems (Alpine) a name with "\r\n.\r\n" ended a posted article on
  the news server's connection, and the rest was read as commands.
  A name with anything but letters, digits, dots, hyphens and
  underscores is now dropped, and the client goes by its address;
  X-NNTP-Posting-Host is also left out when the host has a space or a
  control character.
* LIST ACTIVE, LIST ACTIVE.TIMES and NEWGROUPS collected the list in a
  buffer under the lock and then made a copy of all of it to send it,
  for any client that may list. It is now sent from the buffer itself:
  for a list of 3 million groups (190 MiB) a session's peak memory
  goes from about 370 to 260 MiB.
* With several news servers, the active list could take a group from a
  server it isn't routed to. NewsCache asks each server for "LIST ACTIVE
  <pattern>" per pattern of its Read; the filter for that reply let every
  group through except those it matched against the other servers'
  patterns, and that comparison lost its place after a partial match. With
  one server reading "dk.*,de.alt.binaries.*" and another "de.*", the
  second server's numbers and posting flag for de.alt.binaries.* replaced
  the first's, GROUP and LIST ACTIVE showed them, and the cached records
  above a lower last number were dropped at each refresh. Also, a group
  that another server's Read excludes ("!de.sub.special.*") could be
  missing from the active list, and a group that two servers read with
  the same pattern came with the second server's numbers. Each server's
  reply now keeps only the groups that GROUP takes from that server.
* Group names with a "/" or an empty component (a/b, a.b., .a.b, a..b)
  had the spool directory of a.b: when a news server answered GROUP for
  such a name, its overview records and articles went into a.b's
  database, and readers of a.b got them; such a name also got past an
  exact "!a.b" in a list. They aren't newsgroup names (RFC 5536 3.1.4):
  GROUP and LISTGROUP answer 411 for them now, and the spool refuses
  them.
* newscacheclean neither counted nor removed article files in a group
  directory without a database (left when the database was removed while
  a session still had it open and stored an article, or moved aside as
  corrupt on a full disk). No session can reach them, but they stayed
  outside SpoolSize until a session opened the group again. They are
  removed first now, unless a session has started a new database in the
  directory meanwhile; a file a session stores there while one is being
  removed is kept.
* When memory ran out while a session applied its message-ID history
  updates at the end of an overview reply or a stored chunk, the session
  ended with SIGABRT and a core dump (an exception left a destructor)
  instead of the error reply other failures get. The updates are dropped
  now, with a log message; the history is an index, and a missing entry
  only costs a lookup.
* After a broken connection to a news server, NewsCache selects the
  client's group again on the new connection. That GROUP was sent after
  the set-up guard of the connection was off, so a server that closed the
  connection (or answered 400) at it made NewsCache reconnect from inside
  the reconnect, nested without limit: thousands of connections to the
  server, then the session crashed (stack overflow) without a reply. A
  failed GROUP now ends that connection attempt, the client's command gets
  an error reply after at most Retries attempts, and the session goes on.
  The guard for MODE READER and LIST OVERVIEW.FMT came in 1.3.23 too.
* An XOVER, OVER, XHDR, HDR or LISTGROUP over several chunks of a group
  could leave out records without any error: when another session's
  request moved the group's index up meanwhile (articles far above, or a
  group larger than the index's 10^7 numbers), the chunks that fell below
  it printed nothing, and the list still ended with "." as if complete.
  Those numbers come from the news server now.
* After the clock was put back (a VM whose clock was ahead until NTP
  corrected it), the active list's time stamp lay in the future: the list
  counted as fresh until the clock got there, and a refresh couldn't lower
  the stamp, so new groups stayed invisible and LIST ACTIVE and NEWGROUPS
  stayed as they were for as long as the clock had been ahead (unless
  updatenews -a was run). A stamp still to come now counts as stale, and
  the refresh replaces it. The same holds for the overview's per-chunk
  stamps. Single groups were fixed in 1.3.23 already.
* With Options compress, a valid reply was discarded (the client got 403)
  when more than 64 KiB of compressed data that inflates to nothing (empty
  blocks, which zlib sends for repeated flushes) came before it. The limit
  against a server that sends such blocks for ever is a time now: the
  server's ReadTimeout (5 minutes with ReadTimeout 0) without any content,
  as for a server that sends nothing.
* XOVER, OVER, XHDR, XPAT and HDR across a long stretch of article
  numbers without articles (below the cached part of a group, as in a
  binary group whose low number lags far behind its oldest article)
  asked the news server for one range of 10000 numbers after the other,
  and NEXT and LAST went one number after the other: 10^10 empty numbers
  took a million requests, and the walk went on after the client had
  left, since nothing was written that could fail. After 160000 empty
  numbers in a row they now ask where the next (or previous) article
  is, with LISTGROUP in growing steps as LISTGROUP itself does (about
  120 requests for 10^10 numbers). A connection the client reset (or a
  pipe it closed in inetd mode) ends such a walk at once; otherwise it
  gives up when it has found no article for 5 minutes (a client that
  only closed its sending side may still read the reply).
* NEXT, above the cached part of a group, asked the news server once
  for every number it passed. Looking up one number (NEXT, LAST, STAT)
  now caches the rest of its range of 10000 numbers at once.

Documentation:
* SECURITY.md: how to report a vulnerability privately (GitHub Security
  Advisories, also through the GitHub API with gh api, for scripts and AI
  agents), which versions get security fixes, the scope, and how
  maintainers and collaborators (and their AI agents) coordinate embargoed
  findings, which is not a reporting channel.
* Outdated text: the xdebug session in newscache.conf(5) is a current one
  (201 greeting with the installed version, the entry's file: login,
  PAMServicename); the server Read entry there had a stray fragment and
  pointed to RFC 2980; newscache.conf(5) and newscache.conf-dist said the
  second Timeouts value controls fetching the newsgroup descriptions,
  which NewsCache doesn't do (LIST NEWSGROUPS reads .newsgroups in the
  spool, made by hand); newscache.conf-dist called the cached option "not
  yet implemented" and didn't say that NiceClient has no effect (it is
  kept for compatibility, and commented out in the sample now).
* The manual said that NewsCache uses the most specific AccessList entry
  that matches a client. It uses the first one, in the order of the
  file, as newscache.conf-dist says: put narrower entries before broader
  ones and Default last. The manual and newscache.conf(5) say so now.
* The sample newscache.conf-dist works as /etc/newscache.conf as it is,
  for packages: Admin (shown by HELP) is root@localhost instead of the
  original author's address of 1997, PidFile is /run/newscache.pid,
  LogDirectory /var/log/newscache, and PrefetchFile and the NoServer
  entry are commented out. The NoServer entry made fido*, maus.* and
  z-netz.* (about 3500 groups at news.blueworldhosting.com) answer "411
  no such news group". The comment on PrefetchFile said the daemon
  prefetches when a group is read; only updatenews does. Commented-out
  Server entries show news-fra.pugleaf.net (one of nine read-only text
  servers) and archive.usenet.blueworldhosting.com (old articles).
* COPYING is the current text of the GPL version 2 from gnu.org, and the
  license notices in README, COPYRIGHT and the sources point to
  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/ instead of the FSF's old postal address
  (Fedora's packaging checks flag the old address). The license is
  unchanged: GPL version 2 or later.
* The example "Multiple Upstream NewsServer" in newscache.conf(5) gave
  the client "allow authentication" with "Authentication none", so it
  could do nothing (every command got 500), and put the other servers
  in a second NewsServerList inside the first one. It now has "allow
  read post" and one NewsServerList.
* The man pages and the manual were compared with the code. Retries and
  ReadTimeout are options of a Server entry, not general ones (at the top
  level they are an error); ConnectTimeout, ConnectBackoff,
  LimitGroupSize, User, Password and dontgenmsgid have entries of their
  own in newscache.conf(5), and Hostname too. Its first example had
  options in a Server entry that the parser refuses. Only "Authentication
  unix" without fields requires a login; "allow authentication" only lets
  a client connect so that it can log in. Where no login can add a right,
  a command that needs it gets "502 access denied" (POST "440 posting not
  permitted"). newscache(8) lacked -n in its synopsis, updatenews(8) the
  option -c, newscacheclean(8) -t and -v, and newscache.conf(5) named
  ConfigVersion 3 instead of 5.
* Explain that `PAMServicename` must name an installed service, a missing
  service may fall back to the system's `other` policy depending on PAM
  configuration, values are not quoted, and the `pam_unix` log hint may
  not apply to failures from other modules.
* Clarify supported auth-file password formats, including the weak raw-MD5
  format and platform-dependent `crypt(3)` hashes.
* The manual's example "Options cached,semioffline" didn't parse (the
  options are separated by blanks): it reads "Options cached semioffline".
  The manual shows the package version (it said 0.99.13), describes
  newscacheclean's per-article expiry instead of a plan for it, no longer
  describes the removed configure flag --enable-notcached, and names the
  source directories as they are. newscache(8) shows the sentence on
  TLSCertFile and TLSKeyFile after SIGHUP, which troff swallowed.
  newscache.conf(5) gives the defaults of LogStyle, ListenTo and Admin,
  says that the configuration is read again on SIGHUP, that an empty
  password field or one starting with * or ! locks the user, and how to
  switch off a Commands entry; updatenews(8) says that updatenews ignores
  offline and semioffline and uses a GroupTimeout of 60 seconds.
* README, FEDORA.md, debian/README.Debian and the sample configuration
  now match the code: ReadTimeout and Retries are listed, dontgenmsgid is
  in the sample file, the commands allowed before a login are complete,
  and the Fedora package versions are current.
* The notes in doc/ on PAM, core dumps, stunnel and corrupt databases are
  rewritten for the current parser, the systemd packages, built-in client
  TLS and the connection close after a part-sent reply.
* newscache.conf(5) called a server's Read "wildmat patterns", but the
  groups are routed by names that may end in * and start with !, with the
  longest match winning: a * anywhere matches the rest of the name, and ?
  and [...] are taken literally. The page and the manual say so now, with
  an example of the longest match.

Other changes:
* CI: the macOS jobs (build and test, checkout on a case-insensitive
  file system) run only when CI is started by hand, because GitHub's
  macOS runners often time out in the queue.
* TestLongClientName passes on Arch Linux: in its namespace it also
  reads host names from files first (Arch asks systemd-resolved first,
  which answers 127.0.0.1 with "localhost" itself).
* NewsCache builds on Debian GNU/Hurd: configure defines MAXPATHLEN
  (4096) and MAXHOSTNAMELEN (256) where the system has none, and the
  unit check cCorruptDB skips its file-limit case where RLIMIT_NOFILE
  isn't enforced. cLockDowngrade skips its check that another process
  can read: Hurd's fcntl keeps a write lock when a read lock is asked
  for, so there the shared section that follows an exclusive one holds
  the exclusive lock until it ends. (The e2e tests need Go, which the
  Hurd lacks.)
* The end-to-end tests use nntp-testkit 0.11.0 (still Go 1.26 or later).
* TestSpoolDiskFullDaemon no longer fails on a busy machine (its client
  sat idle past ClientTimeout while the other client worked).
* TestSessionSigchldDefault no longer fails on a busy machine: the
  session of the harness's readiness probe could still be running when
  it counted the daemon's children.
* The PAM and client TLS end-to-end tests no longer fail when two test
  runs share a machine: each PAM service they write to /etc/pam.d has a
  name of its own (it was newscache-e2e-001 in every test, so one run
  read the other's rules), and a TLS listener port taken before
  newscache bound it is replaced by another one, as the tests already
  did for the plain listener.
* GCC 15 with _FORTIFY_SOURCE (Fedora 43's rpm flags) no longer warns
  that the client name lookup's read could overflow its buffer: the
  loop's bound is explicit now.
* make check runs the Go end-to-end tests with a limit of 60 minutes
  (E2E_TIMEOUT sets another). It had go test's default of 10 minutes,
  and the suite takes 8 to 9 minutes on a machine with one core: make
  check failed there with "test timed out after 10m0s".
* The end-to-end test of a TLS client that stops reading allows the
  session 14 seconds to end, not 11: on a busy one-core machine with
  the race detector, the fetches before the reply fills the socket made
  it fail although the session ended in time (11.2 s). The behaviour it
  guards against took about 18 seconds.

Release 1.3.22:
Billy G. - 2026-10-23

Upgrading:
* The upstream news server's password was written to the log (see Bug
  fixes). Check old logs, and change the password if others could read
  them.
* The active database becomes .active64 at the first start; the old
  .active is kept as .active.v1. Older versions can still open the
  spool, but drop each group's cached articles the first time they use
  it; to go back to 1.3.21, rename .active.v1 to .active again.
* "ListenTo DEFAULT" now listens on IPv6 as well as IPv4. Check your
  firewall, or use "ListenTo 0.0.0.0" for IPv4 only.
* ARTICLE, HEAD, BODY and STAT by message-ID, LISTGROUP and XPAT now
  check the Read list of the client's entry, and clients that a login
  can't help get 502 instead of 480.
* LISTGROUP answers "211 count first last group", as RFC 3977 says.
* In the prefetch file of updatenews, a line starting with ! now
  excludes groups; it used to be ignored.
* newscache -p no longer prints the upstream servers' Password; put it
  back into a config made from -p output.
* The spool databases take the disk space of their full size instead of
  being sparse files (with History on, about 8 MB more for the history),
  so that a full disk is an error instead of a crash.
* Packagers: configure.in is now configure.ac (autoconf 2.69 or later)
  and the manual doc/NewsCache.texi; the end-to-end tests need Go 1.26
  and are skipped with an older Go.

New functions:
* XPAT (RFC 2980 2.9): "XPAT header range|<message-id> pattern" lists
  the articles whose header matches the wildmat pattern, in the format of
  XHDR. Like XHDR, it sees the overview fields of every article and all
  headers of the articles in the spool; for other headers of articles
  that are only in the overview, nothing matches. XPAT is in HELP and in
  LIST EXTENSIONS ("pat").
* The prefetch file of updatenews takes wildmat patterns: a line like
  "de.*" or "comp.lang.[cg]o" prefetches every matching group of the
  active list, and a line "!pattern" (or "!group") leaves the matching
  groups out, wherever they come from in the file. Plain group names work
  as before, even for groups the active list doesn't have. Empty lines and
  lines starting with # are skipped, and each group is prefetched once.
  A line starting with ! used to be ignored; it now excludes.
* HDR and LIST HEADERS (RFC 3977 8.5, 8.6). By article number HDR reads
  the overview: Subject, From, Date, Message-ID, References, Xref and the
  metadata :bytes and :lines; other headers get 503, as the RFC asks. By
  message-ID it fetches the article, so every header is there (and
  :bytes). An article without the header gets a line with an empty value,
  and a range without articles 423.
* Articles by message-ID from the spool, with the new option
  "History on" (off by default: then NewsCache behaves as before). It
  keeps a history of the articles in its spool (the directory .history in
  the spool: 16 files of 65536 buckets, keyed by the MD5 hash of the
  Message-ID, like go-pugleaf's and INN's history), and ARTICLE, HEAD,
  BODY and STAT <message-id>, and the message-ID forms of XHDR, XPAT and
  HDR, serve an article from there. An article the spool knows only from
  its overview record is fetched by number and stored, so a crossposted
  article is fetched once (but not into a group the client may not read);
  in offline mode, or with the news server down, articles in the spool
  can be read by message-ID too. Every message-ID request used to go to
  the news server. A spool of an older version gets its entries as
  clients read its articles. newscacheclean drops the entries of groups
  whose database it removed, counts the history toward SpoolSize and
  removes it when it alone takes more than half of SpoolSize (or History
  is off); a damaged history file is renamed to .bad and started anew.
  A history written by a development build since #56 (256 buckets per
  file, "history 1") is moved aside and started anew.

Bug fixes:
* Articles bigger than about 15 KB, which NewsCache stores in their own
  .art file, were never deleted from the spool after they expired on the
  upstream server: the database entry went, the file stayed. Two bugs in
  CNewsgroup: the file name was built as .art<index>d instead of
  .art<number>, and the overview update skipped every article after a range
  it had just fetched, so it never noticed the expired ones there.
* One group name longer than 511 characters in the upstream server's
  active list made NewsCache give up reading the list: clients saw no
  groups at all (LIST empty, GROUP 411 for every group), the database
  stayed locked and the rest of the list was left unread on the
  connection. Such lines are now logged and skipped.
* XOVER and XHDR accepted a range that doesn't start with a number
  ("XOVER -5", "XHDR subject -3") and used an uninitialized first article
  number for it; such ranges now get 501. For an open range ("XOVER 3-")
  the XHDR status line showed the last number as -1, and it showed no
  header name; it now reads
  "221 subject header follows [3-18446744073709551615]".
* The login examples added in 1.3.21 (FEDORA.md and the commented entry in
  the container config) did not require a login: with "allow read post"
  and "Authentication file:", clients that don't log in still read and
  post, and a login only adds rights. The examples now use
  "allow authentication" with "List/Read/PostTo !*" and give the rights in
  the user file, whose format (user:hash:list:read:postto:allow) FEDORA.md
  showed wrongly as user:password. The shipped configs were not affected:
  they let nobody in.
* Before logging in, a client of an entry with Authentication got
  "500 What?" for commands that only a login allows, e.g. every command
  with "allow authentication", or POST with "allow read". Newsreaders wait
  for 480 before they send AUTHINFO, so they couldn't log in. Such commands
  now get "480 Authentication required". Unknown commands, and entries
  without Authentication, still get 500.
* newscache -p and the xdebug command printed "allow authentication" as a
  bare "allow" (and "allow read authentication" as "allow read"). Read
  back as a config, a bare "allow" is allow none, so a login-only entry
  copied from the -p output let nobody in.
* Started as root (by hand, from inetd or from cron), newscache,
  newscacheclean and updatenews switched to Username and Groupname but
  kept root's supplementary groups, group 0 among them; with only a
  Username, also root's primary group. They now set the supplementary
  groups to the user's (initgroups) and, without a Groupname, use the
  user's primary group. A service that systemd starts as root has no
  supplementary groups, so there only the Username-only case was affected.
* In the allow field of an auth-file line (and the last field of
  "Authentication unix:" and "pam:"), "!read", "!post" and "!debug" set
  every other right instead of removing that one: "read,!post" gave a
  logged-in user debug (XDEBUG, which shows the configuration), and "!post"
  didn't take away the post right of the Client entry. They now remove only
  the named right.
* Article numbers above 2^31 were cut down to 32 bits. RFC 3977 stops at
  2^31-1, but the big binary providers passed that long ago (alt.binaries
  groups are above 6*10^10). The numbers went wrong in the upstream
  server's GROUP reply, in OVER and ARTICLE requests to it, in the
  overview records of the spool, in ARTICLE/HEAD/BODY/STAT/XOVER/XHDR and
  in newscacheclean, so such groups showed wrong numbers or an empty
  XOVER. Article numbers are 64-bit now.
  The active database is now the file .active64. An existing .active is
  converted once at the first start (each group keeps its creation time,
  which NEWGROUPS reports) and renamed to .active.v1, which can then be
  deleted.
  A number with trailing characters ("ARTICLE 12x") or one that doesn't
  fit in 64 bits now gets 501; atoi read the leading digits or overflowed.
  On 32-bit systems the spool can't index numbers above 4294967295; such
  articles are passed through from the upstream server without caching.
* STAT with the number of an article that doesn't exist (a gap in the
  group, or past its last article) answered "223 <number>  status",
  without a Message-ID, instead of 423. And a successful STAT <number>
  didn't make that article the current one, so NEXT, LAST and a following
  HEAD or BODY without a number went on from the previous article
  (RFC 3977 6.2.4).
* XHDR copied the header name into a 512-byte buffer on the stack, but a
  command line can be 1024 characters long: a client with read access
  could overflow it with a long header name and crash its session (or
  worse, where the stack isn't protected). The name is now a string.
* An auth-file line with only user and password ("alice:$6$...") was
  refused, so that user could never log in; at least the List field had
  to follow. Such lines are valid now: fields missing at the end add
  nothing, like empty ones, so the user keeps the rights of the Client
  entry. The sample newscache.conf-dist also showed the file format
  without its List and allow fields in one place.
* "Authentication unix" never read /etc/shadow: configure's default
  (--with-shadow=auto) didn't enable shadow password support even where
  shadow.h exists, so the password was compared against the "x" in
  /etc/passwd and every login failed, silently. Shadow support is now on
  by default where shadow.h exists. Note that NewsCache checks passwords
  after it has switched to its Username (news), and only root may read
  /etc/shadow, so unix authentication still can't check local accounts;
  the same is true of PAM with pam_unix. NewsCache now logs why ("cannot
  read the shadow password", and PAM's error with a hint about pam_unix),
  and the manual, newscache.conf(5), newscache.conf-dist and FEDORA.md
  describe the limitation and what works instead: Authentication file:,
  or PAM modules that work without root (pam_sss, pam_ldap, pam_pwdfile).
* newscache -p and "xdebug dump configuration" printed the Password of
  each upstream server in plain text. -p output often ends up in bug
  reports, and xdebug sends it to the client over the network. Both now
  print "# Password set, not shown" instead. Read back as a config, that
  line is a comment, so the upstream login fails (the server answers
  AUTHINFO PASS with an error) until the password is put back.
* newscache, newscacheclean and updatenews copied the -c argument (the
  config file name) with strcpy into a buffer of MAXPATHLEN (4096)
  characters, so a longer argument wrote past its end. Such an argument is
  now refused with "configuration file name is too long" and exit status 1.
  Only whoever starts the programs controls the argument.
* A SpoolDirectory longer than the limit (MAXPATHLEN minus room for the
  longest group name, 3568 characters on Linux) passed the config check,
  and then every session ended with "terminate called after throwing an
  instance of 'AssertionError'", without a reply. It is now a config error
  ("SpoolDirectory too long"), reported at startup by newscache,
  newscacheclean and updatenews (exit status 2), and the check no longer
  depends on --enable-assertions.
* "ListenTo DEFAULT" listened on IPv4 only: the daemon took the first
  wildcard address getaddrinfo returned, which is 0.0.0.0 on Linux, so
  IPv6 clients could not connect. DEFAULT (and "DEFAULT:port") now listens
  on one IPv6 socket that accepts IPv4 clients too, and falls back to IPv4
  where the host has no IPv6. IPv4 clients are still logged, matched
  against Client entries and named in NNTP-Posting-Host by their plain
  IPv4 address. To listen on IPv4 only, use "ListenTo 0.0.0.0".
* The pid file (PidFile) was never removed: after the daemon stopped, it
  still held the old pid. newscache now removes it when it stops on
  SIGTERM or SIGINT, if it still holds its own pid. The file is written as
  root, before the switch to Username, so removing it needs a directory
  that Username can write to (e.g. /run/newscache owned by news); in /run
  itself the file is emptied instead. The configs for systemd (Debian
  package, FEDORA.md) and the container no longer set a PidFile, since the
  service manager knows the pid; without PidFile no file is written and
  nothing is logged (it used to warn "cannot open pid file").
* Overview records from the upstream server were converted with the tab
  positions in a fixed array of 256, so a record with more fields than
  that overflowed the stack. Servers replace tabs in header values with
  spaces, so only a broken or hostile upstream sends such a record. The
  conversion only runs when the upstream's overview format differs from
  NewsCache's.
* The size fields that RFC 3977 servers list as ":bytes" and ":lines" in
  LIST OVERVIEW.FMT matched none of NewsCache's fields ("Bytes:",
  "Lines:"), so with such a server the Bytes and Lines fields of XOVER
  were empty. And a field the upstream sends with its name (e.g.
  "Subject:full") lost the name but kept the colon (": text").
* LISTGROUP with a group the client may not read (Read in its Client
  entry) answered 480, but only after it had made that group the current
  one; XOVER, XHDR, ARTICLE <number> and the other commands for the
  current group then read it, so "LISTGROUP secret.group" followed by
  "XOVER" showed the group. LISTGROUP now checks first.
* ARTICLE, HEAD, BODY and STAT with a message-ID didn't check the Read
  list at all: a client that may read only some groups got any article
  whose message-ID it knew. The article must now be in at least one group
  the client may read (its Newsgroups header).
* A group or article the client may not read got 480 even when no login
  could help (no Authentication in its entry, or already logged in). It
  now gets 502, as RFC 4643 says; 480 stays for clients that can log in.
* The PostTo check split the Newsgroups header wrongly: with three or more
  groups a name was checked with the following ones appended, which
  matched only if PostTo listed the same groups in the same order, and
  the space after a comma was kept, so such posts were refused. A refused
  group now gets 441 once the client is logged in (or can't log in), not
  480.
* The first XOVER (or XHDR, LISTGROUP, STAT, NEXT, LAST) on a group made
  NewsCache synchronize the whole group, whatever range the client asked
  for: a LISTGROUP of the whole group from the upstream server, the
  overview records of every article it didn't have, a 1-byte-per-number
  table in memory and a spool index of 8 bytes for every number from the
  group's first to its last, written out in full. For a binary group with
  numbers from 60000000000 to 67486767815 that is a 57 GB index and 7.5 GB
  of memory, so such groups failed ("412 operation failed") or filled the
  disk. Groups without caching (Options not-cached) fetched the overview
  of the whole group at every GROUP.
  NewsCache now asks for the numbers a client asks for, in steps of 10000
  ("LISTGROUP group a-b", RFC 3977, then OVER for the missing records), and
  compares each step with the upstream server again only after its
  GroupTimeout. The spool's index covers what clients read, at most the
  newest 10000000 numbers of a group; articles further back are passed
  through without caching. LISTGROUP from a client passes the upstream
  server's list on as it arrives, takes a range ("LISTGROUP group 100-200")
  and answers "211 count first last group" as RFC 3977 says (it was
  "211 list of article numbers follow"). updatenews -g and -o prefetch the
  newest 10000000 numbers of each group. Spools of older versions keep
  working. Older versions open the new spools, but drop a group's cached
  articles the first time they use it (its index now starts above the
  server's first number, which they take for a renumbered group); the
  .art files of big articles stay until newscacheclean needs the space.
  When the server's last number goes down (its newest articles cancelled
  or expired, or a server farm whose frontends lag behind), only the
  cached articles above it are dropped; numbers far above, up to 2^64,
  can't make a session loop.
  An OVER/XOVER range without articles (423, or 420 from older servers)
  made NewsCache assume the upstream server has no OVER, then no XOVER,
  and give up on the overview; it now means no records.
* CAPABILITIES listed READER, POST and, for entries with Authentication,
  AUTHINFO USER to every client: also to clients without the right to
  read or post, and AUTHINFO also after the login (RFC 4643 says not
  then). It now lists only what the client can do at that moment, and
  adds HDR, OVER and LIST HEADERS for readers.
* XPAT with a message-ID ("XPAT subject <id> *") returned the header of
  any article whose message-ID the client knew, also in groups its Read
  list doesn't allow (XPAT came in this release, the Read check of
  ARTICLE <id> after it). It now checks like ARTICLE, as XHDR and HDR do.
* XHDR takes a message-ID ("XHDR subject <id>", RFC 2980 2.6); it
  answered 501. The line starts with the message-ID, and no group needs
  to be selected.
* A damaged database in the spool (a group's .db, or .active64) could
  crash NewsCache. Damage behind the header crashed every session that
  touched the group (SIGSEGV), a short or damaged .active64 aborted every
  session before the greeting, a short .db gave "412 operation failed"
  for good, and newscacheclean ended with SIGABRT at a damaged .db, before
  it had removed anything. The offsets and sizes in these files are now
  checked before they are used; a damaged file is moved to name.bad (one
  error line in the log), a new one is started and filled from the
  upstream server again, and the command that ran into it is retried.
  newscacheclean goes on with the other groups and removes the .bad
  files. A command that fails with any other unexpected error now gets a
  503 instead of ending the session (e.g. a stored article that doesn't
  parse; in inetd mode that aborted with SIGABRT).
  Processes that run into the same damaged file at once move it aside
  only once (under a lock on its directory) and all go on with the same
  new file; one could move another's new file aside, and the updates
  made there were lost. Opening a database checks it under a lock, so a
  sound file that another process is writing to is no longer taken for
  damaged. If no new file can be started (no file descriptor left, no
  space), the database answers errors until it can, instead of using
  the old mapping (a crash).
* CachePort never worked: the check of its first character was the wrong
  way round since the IPv6 support of 2008, so "CachePort nntp" listened
  on the service "ntp" read as a number and "CachePort #12000" passed the
  "#" on, and the daemon couldn't start. It also took precedence over
  ListenTo, which the documentation says it doesn't. It now takes a
  service name, a number or #number, and is ignored (with a log line)
  when ListenTo is set; newscache -p no longer adds a ListenTo line that
  would hide it.
* DATE with an argument answered "501 Syntax: group Newsgroup"; it now
  gives DATE's own syntax.
* An upstream server that returns at most so many overview records per
  OVER, whatever the range asked for, cut groups short: NewsCache asks
  for a run of up to 10000 numbers, took everything after the last record
  it got for articles without a record, and clients saw only the first
  part of the range (until the next sync, which went the same way). It
  now asks again after the last record received until the range is done.
  A server that returns whole ranges costs at most one more request
  (answered 423), when a range ends in numbers without articles.
* When the upstream server asked for the login again in the middle of a
  session (480, as servers do when a login times out), NewsCache took the
  480 to an OVER for "no OVER", then the one to XOVER for "no XOVER", and
  gave up on the overview: clients got an empty XOVER without an error
  until the session ended; other commands failed with the 480. NewsCache
  logged in only when it connected. It now logs in again (with the
  server's User and Password) and sends the command once more.
* The password of the upstream news server was written to the log:
  every command to the server is logged at level info ("RServer::issue:
  issue ..."), "authinfo pass <password>" too. With syslog that is the
  news facility, which Debian's default rsyslog also writes to
  /var/log/syslog (readable by group adm), and the systemd journal; with
  --without-syslog, newscache.log. AUTHINFO to the server is now logged
  without its arguments, as the client's AUTHINFO already was. Check old
  logs, and change the password if they were readable by others.
* Once a client had read an article (up to 15 kB, kept in the group
  database), its overview record was made from the article: :lines was
  empty when the article has no Lines header (most articles; RFC 5536
  made it obsolete), XHDR Lines gave "(none)" and HDR :lines nothing, and
  :bytes counted the dots added for dot-stuffing (RFC 3977 counts the
  article without them). :lines now counts the body lines, and :bytes
  leaves those dots out, also for HDR :bytes <message-id>.
* With several news servers, the Xref of an article lost the groups that
  another server serves (their numbers are the other server's), but the
  Xref of its overview record kept them: a newsreader that marks
  crossposts read by the overview's Xref marked articles of the other
  group that have those numbers on the other server. Overview records
  from a server now get the same Xref as its articles.
* A full spool crashed NewsCache with SIGBUS: the databases (group
  databases, the active list, the history) grow with ftruncate, which
  leaves a hole, and are written through mmap, where a hole the file
  system cannot fill is a signal, not an error. With History on it
  happened at the first history file. The space is now taken when a
  database grows (posix_fallocate, or zeros written where there is
  none; the file size limit counts too), so a full disk is an error,
  and clients hardly notice it: articles and overview records that
  can't be kept are passed on from the news server (a 423 or a cut
  XOVER before), the active list is read to its end so the connection
  to the news server stays in step, and every database lock is given
  back when the error comes (a lock left held would have kept every
  other client of that database waiting until the session ended).
  Nothing is left half written: a database record is replaced only
  after the new one is allocated, and an article whose own file (over
  15 kB) could not be written completely was kept cut off and served
  so from then on; such a file is now removed. A file that is empty
  or can't be opened (no file descriptor left) is no longer served as
  an article ("503 internal error"): the article is fetched again.
  The databases now take the disk space of their size (the history's
  16 files about 8 MB) instead of being sparse.

Other changes:
* Spool, config and log file paths are built with bounded snprintf instead
  of sprintf, group2dir() checks the group name's length, and a group whose
  spool path wouldn't leave room for its article files is refused. These
  overflows needed an extremely long SpoolDirectory or LogDirectory, so
  they were not reachable from outside. The buffers for generated
  Message-IDs are sized from the host name limit. Together this removes
  the -Wformat-overflow and -Wformat-truncation warnings.
* Debian packaging brought up to date for Debian 13 and Ubuntu 24.04:
  "dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b" builds a newscache package with GnuTLS
  and PAM that runs as user news under systemd, cleans the spool daily
  with a timer and ships an /etc/newscache.conf that lets nobody in. See
  debian/README.Debian.
* The info manual has a dir entry, and newscache.conf(5) a whatis line.
* The end-to-end tests use nntp-testkit 0.5.0 and need Go 1.26 or later.
  With an older Go, make check now skips them with a message, instead of
  having Go try to download a newer toolchain (which fails offline). Set
  GOTOOLCHAIN to let Go fetch one anyway. The fake upstream server can
  now behave like real servers that don't follow RFC 3977 to the letter,
  and tests/e2e/upstream_test.go runs NewsCache against them: LISTGROUP
  that rejects or ignores a range, only OVER or only XOVER, 420 or an
  empty 224 for an empty range, a last number that goes down (cancels,
  lagging frontends of a server farm), groups whose articles all expired
  ("211 0 0 0"), a connection limit, unusual message-IDs, and clients
  that pipeline their commands. tests/e2e/upstreams_test.go adds a group
  of 10^7 articles (generated on demand), an overview record whose
  Message-ID is not the article's, and two servers that number the same
  group differently; tests/e2e/spoolfull_test.go runs NewsCache with few
  file descriptors, with writes that fail (ulimit -f) and on a spool that
  fills up (a small tmpfs, where user namespaces are allowed).
* doc/websites/ holds an offline copy, from the Wayback Machine, of the
  two original NewsCache websites (Thomas Gschwind, TU Wien; Herbert
  Straub, linuxhacker.at), the 1.2rc6 tarball, patches and man pages they
  link to, and an index page in every folder so it can be served as a
  website.
* The compiler warnings are gone, apart from autoconf/automake ones and one
  that the XOVER range fix removes (GCC 16 with Fedora's flags: 56 before).
  Most were harmless (catch by value, unused variables, a never-NULL array
  checked against NULL). A few fixes change behaviour in cases that
  shouldn't happen: a corrupt active database with a broken record chain
  no longer dereferences NULL, a failed read of an upstream reply no longer
  checks an uninitialized status, updatenews no longer reads an undefined
  exit status when wait() fails, LimitGroupSize ignores a group whose
  last article number is below its first, and builds without syslog stop
  with an error if LogDirectory is too long for the log file name.
* New option AuthFailureDelay: the seconds NewsCache waits before it
  answers a failed login, multiplied by the number of failed logins in the
  connection. The default stays 15 (15, 30, 45 seconds); 0 answers at
  once, the maximum is 3600. It was fixed at 15 seconds.
* configure.in is now configure.ac and the manual doc/NewsCache.texi
  (was doc/NewsCache.texinfo), and the obsolete autoconf and automake
  macros are replaced, so autogen (autoreconf) and configure run without
  warnings (21 with autoconf 2.73 and automake 1.18 before). The version
  is set in AC_INIT now. PACKAGE and VERSION, the tarball name and the
  configure options are unchanged; config.h also defines PACKAGE_NAME,
  PACKAGE_VERSION and the like now, and configure --help names the issue
  tracker. Autoconf 2.69 or later is needed. Packagers who patch
  configure.in or refer to NewsCache.texinfo need to follow the renames.
* The release tarball has the notes in doc/ again (newscache-pam.txt,
  newscache-stunnel-ssl.txt, ...), which README.md points to, and the NNTP
  specifications there. doc/Makefile.am had no backslash after
  "EXTRA_DIST =", so its list was ignored, and it named two drafts that
  were no longer in the tree.
* Documentation: the manual's chapter "Access Control" described only
  libwrap; it now explains the AccessList (nothing is allowed by default,
  Client and Default entries, rights, groups, logins) with an example.
  The login example in newscache.conf-dist made the login optional
  ("allow read post"); it now uses "allow authentication" with the rights
  in the user file, as FEDORA.md does. debian/README.Debian still said an
  auth-file line needs the list field. newscache(8) lacked -n and what -c
  does. The FAQ says what to do after moving groups to another news
  server: remove their databases, or NewsCache may serve the old server's
  cached articles under the new server's numbers.

Release 1.3.21:
Billy G. - 2026-10-20

Upgrading:
* The shipped configs no longer let anyone in. If you copy
  newscache.conf-dist or use the container image, add a Client entry for
  your clients' addresses or networks, or an entry with Authentication
  (login), before Default.
* A Client entry with a /prefix after something that isn't an IP address
  (e.g. a host name like news.example.org/24) is now a config error, so
  such a config no longer loads. Before, the entry silently never matched.
  Check with newscache -p before restarting.

Bug fixes:
* The sample newscache.conf-dist allowed inetd clients (Client stdin, with
  debug) and clients from localhost, and the container config allowed
  loopback and all private networks. Both now only contain
  Default { allow none }, with the old entries as commented examples, so
  nobody may read or post until a Client entry (address or CIDR) or a login
  allows it. FEDORA.md does the same and shows how to allow clients.
* newscache -p and the xdebug command printed AccessList entries for an
  address or network (e.g. Client 192.168.0.0/255.255.0.0) as "Default",
  without the address; read back as a config, such an entry would have let
  every client in. They now print as Client 192.168.0.0/16.
* On hosts where getaddrinfo considers IPv6 unavailable (seen in Docker
  containers), an IPv6 Client entry such as 2001:db8::/32 was not parsed as
  an address and silently became a host name that never matched. Addresses
  are now parsed without AI_ADDRCONFIG. A /prefix after something that
  isn't an IP address (e.g. 300.1.1.1/8) is now a config error instead of
  an entry that silently never matches.

Other changes:
* Dockerfile for Docker and Podman (also Docker Desktop and Podman Desktop on
  Windows): builds on Ubuntu 24.04, runs as user news in the foreground and
  logs to the container output. Its config reads from
  news.blueworldhosting.com over TLS and lets nobody in until you mount a
  config with a Client entry or a login. CI builds the image and checks both.
* CI also builds and runs make check on Linux ARM64, Alpine 3.24 (musl),
  Debian 13 and FreeBSD.
* README.md: how to run NewsCache in a container and on Windows (WSL 2).

Release 1.3.20:
Billy G. - 2026-10-20

Upgrading: with Options ssl or starttls, NewsCache now verifies the server's
TLS certificate and drops the connection if it is invalid, expired or for
another name, or if STARTTLS is refused. Check the log after upgrading. For a
server with a private CA set TLSCAFile; Options noverify accepts any
certificate, without protection against a man-in-the-middle.

Bug fixes:
* TLS to upstream servers (Options ssl and starttls) now verifies the
  server's certificate: it must chain to a trusted CA, be valid and match the
  host name in the Server line. Before, any certificate was accepted, so
  anyone in the middle could impersonate the server.
* STARTTLS no longer falls back to plain text. If the server refused
  STARTTLS, NewsCache continued without TLS and sent User/Password in plain
  text, which anyone in the middle could force; now the connection is
  dropped. A failed TLS handshake after STARTTLS also ends the connection.
* A NewsCache built without GnuTLS ignored Options ssl and starttls and
  talked to such a server in plain text, login included. It now refuses to
  connect to it and logs why.
* TLS uses the system's trust store (it was a fixed Debian path, missing on
  Fedora and macOS), sends the host name as SNI, has a handshake timeout and
  logs why a handshake or verification failed.
* newscache -p left the server options ssl, starttls and dontgenmsgid out of
  its output, although they were parsed and used.

Other changes:
* New server settings: TLSCAFile (CA certificates for servers with a
  private CA) and Options noverify (accept any certificate, logged as a
  warning). The man page now documents ssl and starttls, and the sample
  config no longer lists not-ssl and not-starttls, which never existed.
* Bug reports and the home page point to
  https://github.com/go-while/NewsCache instead of the dead members.aon.at,
  linuxhacker.at and TU Wien sites and mailing lists: in the error messages
  that ask for a report, the man pages, README, INSTALL (whose socket++
  instructions were obsolete), the texinfo manual, doc/ and debian/.
* NVClasses: NVlist.{h,cc} is now NVListBase.{h,cc} (class NVListBase).
  NVlist.h and NVList.h clashed on case-insensitive file systems, so
  checkouts on Windows and macOS were broken. The spool format is unchanged.
* src/Config.{h,cc} is now src/NewsCacheConfig.{h,cc} (the class is still
  Config). On case-insensitive file systems, #include "config.h" in src/
  found src/Config.h instead of the header that configure generates.
* NewsCache builds on macOS: configure checks for crypt.h (macOS and the
  BSDs declare crypt() in unistd.h), and wildmat.c no longer uses a K&R
  function definition, which C23 compilers such as Xcode's clang reject.
* CI: build and make check on macOS, and a check on Windows and macOS that
  the tree checks out cleanly on a case-insensitive file system.
* The sample newscache.conf-dist and FEDORA.md use news.blueworldhosting.com
  (port 563, TLS) instead of the long-gone TU Wien servers, and README.md
  lists public news servers.
* FEDORA.md: all steps tested on Fedora 43, 44 and 45.
* Removed stamp-h.in (an automake leftover) and the TODO file from 2013;
  its still-open items are in REVIEW.md.

Release 1.3.19:
Billy G. - 2026-10-19

Bug fixes:
* NewsCache crashed at startup ("buffer overflow detected" with
  _FORTIFY_SOURCE, memory corruption without) on hosts whose fully
  qualified domain name is 64 characters or longer, as on cloud VMs such
  as GitHub's runners. The host name buffers were MAXHOSTNAMELEN (64)
  bytes, which is the kernel host name limit, not the DNS limit; they are
  now 256 bytes and the copies are bounded. Found by the new CI.

Other changes:
* CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml): make check on Ubuntu (default with the
  race detector and make distcheck, file logging with debug, and
  AddressSanitizer) and on Fedora 43, 44 and 45 with Fedora's packaging
  flags.
* README: says that NewsCache is developed on GitHub and that the
  Subversion repository is archived (SVN r81 added a note there).

Release 1.3.18:
Billy G. - 2026-10-19

Bug fixes:
* Authentication: a missing optional field was read past the end of the
  field list, and the client session crashed after a successful login.
  This happened with "Authentication unix" or "pam" without the
  ":list:read:postto:flags" fields (documented in newscache.conf(5)), and
  with auth-file lines shorter than user:password:list:read:postto:flags.
  Missing fields are now empty.
* MD5 password hashes in auth files (base64, 24 characters ending in "==")
  crashed the session on 64-bit systems. md5lib used unsigned long for its
  32-bit words, which is 64 bits there, so computing the hash overran a
  stack buffer (and gave a wrong hash). It now uses uint32_t. Affects every
  release since at least 1.2rc6 on 64-bit systems; crypt() hashes such as
  $6$ were not affected. Found by CodeQL.
* DATE and NEWGROUPS use gmtime_r/localtime_r, and NEWGROUPS checks their
  result instead of dereferencing a NULL pointer on failure.

Other changes:
* CodeQL code scanning (.github/workflows/codeql.yml) runs on pushes and
  pull requests to main and weekly.

Tests:
* End-to-end tests for file authentication: a short auth-file line, an MD5
  hash, and a wrong password.
* End-to-end tests for DATE and NEWGROUPS.

Release 1.3.17:
Billy G. - 2026-10-18

First release from https://github.com/go-while/NewsCache, which continues
NewsCache with Christof Meerwald's agreement. It is SVN trunk r80 (1.3.16)
plus the fixes and tests below.

Bug fixes:
* Access list: a Client entry with a plain IP address and no mask (for
  example "Client 10.1.2.3") had prefix length 0 and matched every client of
  that address family. It now means exactly that host. Affects 1.3.ipv6 to
  1.3.16 (since the IPv6 support); configs with a mask or a host name are not
  affected, and 1.2rc6 matched such entries by name only.
* Access list: entries for one exact host (a.b.c.d/32, /255.255.255.255,
  /128) never matched, including the example in newscache.conf(5). A prefix
  longer than the address is now a syntax error.
* SIGHUP with a broken newscache.conf no longer kills the daemon; it logs the
  error and keeps the running configuration.
* Upstream articles without an Xref header are served instead of failing with
  423/430.
* "400 too many users" is no longer followed by a NUL byte.
* "502 You have no permission to talk" ends with CRLF instead of a bare LF.
* Log file timestamps (builds without syslog) showed the previous month.
* newscacheclean: "ng == NULL | nr < firstnr" read an uninitialized variable
  (| instead of ||); the result was the same.
* The sample newscache.conf-dist loads again: BindFrom only takes numeric
  addresses since the IPv6 support, and the sample used a host name.
* make dist works again (a misspelled header name in NServer-0.9.1, there
  since 1.2rc6), and make distcheck passes: the test programs are now
  check_PROGRAMS, so make clean removes them.
* configure.in carries the release version again; it still said 1.3.ipv6,
  so 1.3.16 greeted clients with "NewsCache 1.3.ipv6".

Tests:
* make check passes again (cArticle, cNewsgroupFilter and cConfig were broken).
* New end-to-end tests in tests/e2e (Go, using
  github.com/go-while/nntp-testkit) run the real newscache binary against a
  fake upstream server. make check runs them when Go is installed and skips
  them otherwise.

Documentation:
* README.md: project history, building, and a check of the old documentation.
* FEDORA.md: building, testing and installing on Fedora, with a systemd unit
  and a cleanup timer.

